(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.73ee74990e.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"In a study of the embryonic gut tube, an investigator maps which adult structures receive their blood supply from the artery of the midgut. Which of the following structures belongs to this territory?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Upper rectum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut begins just caudal to the liver bud and ends at the junction of the right two-thirds and left one-third of the transverse colon; it forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon, all supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum therefore lies in the midgut territory. The gastric fundus and the pancreatic tail are foregut derivatives supplied by the celiac artery (the pancreatic tail through the splenic artery). The sigmoid colon and upper rectum are hindgut derivatives supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric supply of the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA; the midgut runs from the lower duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: map visceral pain and ischemia to the gut region and its artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the duodenum receives arterial branches from two separate aortic trunks. Which feature of its development explains this?","choices":{"A":"Dual midgut and hindgut origin","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Dual foregut and midgut origin","D":"Secondary retroperitoneal fixation","E":"Origin from the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenum forms from the terminal part of the foregut and the cephalic part of the midgut. Because the foregut is supplied by the celiac artery and the midgut by the superior mesenteric artery, the duodenum receives branches of both: the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the celiac system and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. The hindgut contributes nothing to the duodenum. Rotation around the SMA describes the midgut loop, not the source of duodenal arteries. Secondary retroperitoneal fixation by Toldt fascia changes position, not blood supply. The hepatic diverticulum gives rise to the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut-midgut junction lies at the ampulla of Vater, where the bile and pancreatic ducts enter (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: pancreaticoduodenal arcades link the celiac and SMA territories as a collateral route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"An embryology student traces the boundary between the foregut-derived and midgut-derived parts of the duodenum. Which landmark marks this boundary?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Duodenojejunal flexure","C":"Minor duodenal papilla","D":"Superior duodenal flexure","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The junction between the foregut and midgut lies just distal to the origin of the liver bud, which in the adult corresponds to the entrance of the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. Duodenum proximal to the papilla is foregut and celiac supplied; duodenum distal to it is midgut and SMA supplied. The pyloric sphincter and the superior duodenal flexure lie within the foregut portion. The duodenojejunal flexure lies within the midgut. The minor papilla, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains, lies proximal to the major papilla and does not mark the junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (normal gastrointestinal embryology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut runs from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the pancreatic duct and common bile duct insertion (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: obstruction distal to this point produces bilious vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula most often results from which developmental error?","choices":{"A":"Posterior deviation of the septum","B":"Failure of the lung bud to form","C":"Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","D":"Arrested lengthening of the esophagus","E":"Abnormal migration of neural crest cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman attributes esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula to spontaneous posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum or to a mechanical force pushing the dorsal foregut wall anteriorly, so the septum partitions the foregut incompletely. Failure of the lung bud to form causes pulmonary agenesis rather than a fistula. Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds causes congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Arrested lengthening of the esophagus pulls the stomach into the hiatus as a congenital hiatal hernia. Abnormal neural crest migration produces aganglionic segments such as Hirschsprung disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, pathogenesis of esophageal atresia and TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most common variant is esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, which presents with polyhydramnios and choking at the first feed; associated with VACTERL (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an NG tube that will not pass into the stomach confirms the atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which variant of esophageal atresia or tracheoesophageal fistula is the most common?","choices":{"A":"Isolated atresia without a fistula","B":"Upper pouch fistula with distal atresia","C":"H-type fistula without atresia","D":"Proximal atresia with distal fistula","E":"Separate fistulas at both segments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 90% of cases are a blind proximal esophageal pouch with the distal esophagus joined to the trachea just above the bifurcation. Isolated atresia without a fistula and the H-type fistula without atresia each account for about 4%. A proximal fistula with a blind distal segment and a double fistula each account for about 1%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, anatomic variations and frequency); Langman Ch. 15 (Fig. 15.7, variants in order of frequency). First Aid 2025 GI gives 85% for this variant; Langman and the lecture give 90%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EA with distal TEF is the most common type; air entering the stomach through the fistula produces a prominent gastric bubble, whereas pure EA gives a gasless abdomen (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: aspiration of gastric acid through the distal fistula causes chemical pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, why is esophageal atresia associated with polyhydramnios?","choices":{"A":"Fetal kidneys produce excess urine","B":"Fluid leaks from the fistula into the lungs","C":"Swallowed fluid does not reach the intestine","D":"Amniotic membranes secrete more fluid","E":"Placental transfer of fluid to the mother falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid, which is absorbed in the intestine. With esophageal atresia the fluid does not pass the blind pouch into the stomach and gut, so it accumulates in the amniotic sac. Polyhydramnios here is a problem of fetal clearance, not of fetal urine production, which is unchanged. Fluid entering the airway through a fistula is not a route of amniotic clearance. The membranes and placenta are not the site of the defect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia prevents passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyhydramnios points to an inability to swallow or absorb amniotic fluid, as in esophageal or duodenal atresia; oligohydramnios points to absent fetal urine (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: polyhydramnios on prenatal ultrasound should prompt evaluation for upper GI atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, after the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis, the left vagus nerve innervates which part of the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Posterior wall","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Greater curvature","D":"Anterior wall","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 90-degree clockwise rotation turns the original left side of the stomach to face anteriorly and the right side to face posteriorly. The left vagus nerve, which ran along the left side, therefore comes to innervate the anterior wall, and the right vagus nerve innervates the posterior wall. The lesser and greater curvatures form from the slower-growing anterior and faster-growing posterior walls, not from vagal territory. The pyloric antrum moves upward and to the right during rotation around the anteroposterior axis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the longitudinal axis); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise; left vagus becomes anterior and right vagus becomes posterior (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: the anterior vagal trunk lies on the front of the distal esophagus during vagotomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the greater curvature of the stomach forms because one of the original walls grows faster than the opposite wall during rotation. Which wall is this?","choices":{"A":"Original anterior wall","B":"Original left lateral wall","C":"Original right lateral wall","D":"Original cranial wall","E":"Original posterior wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During rotation the original posterior wall of the stomach grows faster than the anterior wall, forming the greater curvature; the slower-growing anterior wall forms the lesser curvature. The original left and right walls become the anterior and posterior surfaces after the 90-degree clockwise rotation and carry the left and right vagal trunks. The original cranial end becomes the cardia and shifts during rotation around the anteroposterior axis; it does not form a curvature. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, differential wall growth and curvatures); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal mesogastrium attaches to the greater curvature and becomes the greater omentum; ventral mesogastrium attaches to the lesser curvature and forms the lesser omentum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: these attachments define the peritoneal ligaments seen in gastric surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the spleen primordium appears during the fifth week of development between the two leaves of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ventral mesogastrium","B":"Dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Lesser omentum","D":"Dorsal mesoduodenum","E":"Septum transversum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spleen primordium arises as a mesodermal proliferation between the leaves of the dorsal mesogastrium. Gastric rotation swings it to the left, and the dorsal mesogastrium between spleen and stomach becomes the gastrosplenic ligament, while the part between spleen and left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament. The ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the lesser omentum and falciform ligament and contains the developing liver. The dorsal mesoduodenum receives the dorsal pancreatic bud. The septum transversum is the mesoderm into which the liver bud grows. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach mesenteries and spleen).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen arises in the dorsal mesogastrium and is mesodermal, yet it has a foregut blood supply through the splenic branch of the celiac trunk (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments are divided during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Congenital duodenal atresia most often results from which process?","choices":{"A":"Vascular accident with resorption","B":"Abnormal rotation of the ventral bud","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Posterior deviation of the septum","E":"Hypertrophy of circular muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenal lumen is obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalized by vacuolization; when recanalization fails, the lumen stays occluded, producing atresia, and when it is incomplete it produces stenosis. Vascular accidents with resorption of bowel cause jejunal and ileal atresia. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas that encircles the duodenum. Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal atresia. Hypertrophy of circular muscle at the pylorus causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failure of recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (upper duodenal atresias probably from lack of recanalization; Langman attributes atresia from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents, while the lecture applies failed recanalization to the duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = failure to recanalize from the solid cord stage, double bubble on radiograph, associated with Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia = vascular disruption (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: check infants with trisomy 21 for duodenal atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"During normal development of the duodenum, epithelial proliferation briefly converts the lumen into a solid cord. According to Langman, which event follows this solid stage?","choices":{"A":"Vacuole formation and coalescence","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Fusion of the pancreatic buds","D":"Herniation into the umbilical cord","E":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After the solid stage, vacuoles form within the epithelial cord through apoptosis and cleft formation and then coalesce into a continuous lumen, restoring a patent tube. Rotation around the SMA axis and physiologic herniation into the umbilical cord are events of the midgut loop. Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds follows rotation of the duodenum but does not reopen its lumen. The hepatic diverticulum grows out from the foregut before this stage and forms the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, normal duodenal recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (solid stage and cavity formation, Fig. 15.18).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure of this vacuolization gives duodenal atresia or stenosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the same failure in the esophagus produces esophageal stenosis, usually in the lower third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"In an infant with congenital duodenal obstruction, the vomitus is bile stained. Where is the obstruction most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Proximal to the major papilla","B":"Distal to the major papilla","C":"At the pyloric sphincter","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile enters the duodenum at the major papilla, so obstruction distal to it produces bilious vomiting; the lecture notes that about 80% to 85% of duodenal obstructions lie distal to the papilla. Obstruction proximal to the papilla, at the pylorus, at the gastroesophageal junction, or in the antrum keeps bile out of the vomitus, which is nonbilious, as in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, clinical and radiographic presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (entrance of the bile duct at the major papilla); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilious emesis in a neonate signals obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater, such as duodenal atresia or malrotation with volvulus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis results mainly from enlargement of which tissue layer of the pylorus?","choices":{"A":"Circular muscularis externa","B":"Pyloric mucosal epithelium","C":"Submucosal connective tissue","D":"Antral muscularis mucosae","E":"Visceral peritoneal serosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyloric stenosis occurs when the circular musculature of the pylorus, and to a lesser degree the longitudinal musculature, hypertrophies and narrows the pyloric lumen. The lecture adds that the circular layer of the muscularis propria often thickens by 50% to 100%. The mucosa and submucosa may become edematous and inflamed, but they are secondary changes. The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer within the mucosa that does not form the obstructing mass. The serosa is a covering layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, underlying developmental abnormality); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (hyperplasia of the pyloric muscularis propria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the pyloric sphincter muscle produce the palpable olive; treat with pyloromyotomy (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the myotomy divides the hypertrophied muscle down to the submucosa and spares the mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"An investigator examines pyloric muscle from infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and finds few neurons expressing the enzyme that produces the main relaxing transmitter of the sphincter. Which transmitter is deficient?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Substance P","C":"Serotonin","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pyloric relaxation is mediated by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic neurons that release nitric oxide made by neuronal nitric oxide synthase. In hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, neuronal nitric oxide synthase is markedly deficient in the pyloric muscle plexus, so the sphincter fails to relax, and persistent contraction drives work hypertrophy and narrowing. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract GI smooth muscle. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and secretory mediator in the enteric nervous system. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers relaxes gut wall muscle but contracts sphincters. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, nitric oxide synthase deficiency). Among the staged sources this mechanism is stated by the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of NO-mediated relaxation also underlies achalasia, where NO and VIP inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus degenerate at the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: think of failed relaxation when a sphincter region thickens in an infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, postnatal exposure to which drug substantially increases the risk of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Amoxicillin","B":"Gentamicin","C":"Ceftriaxone","D":"Metronidazole","E":"Erythromycin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Langman notes that treatment with erythromycin in the newborn period substantially increases the risk of pyloric stenosis, suggesting that the defect can develop after birth; Robbins and the lecture extend the association to azithromycin, either given directly or through breast milk in the first 2 weeks of life. Amoxicillin, gentamicin, ceftriaxone, and metronidazole are commonly used in neonates but are not macrolides and are not linked to pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, environmental and medication exposure); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is more common in firstborn males and is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: weigh this risk when choosing pertussis prophylaxis for a young infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a bleeding posterior ulcer of the duodenal bulb, and the gastroduodenal artery is surgically ligated. Afterward the duodenum distal to the major papilla remains well perfused. This part of the duodenum is perfused mainly by branches of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Common hepatic artery","C":"Left gastric artery","D":"Right gastroepiploic artery","E":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The duodenum distal to the major papilla is midgut and is supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, so it stays perfused after the gastroduodenal artery is tied. The common hepatic artery gives rise to the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the foregut part of the duodenum through the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. The right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of the ligated gastroduodenal artery. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum supplied by both celiac and superior mesenteric arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac trunk branches are the common hepatic, splenic, and left gastric arteries, and the pancreaticoduodenal arcades join the celiac and SMA territories (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, and the arcades protect the duodenum when it is ligated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman develops left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools after a prolonged episode of hypotension. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from the distal third of the transverse colon through the sigmoid colon, with a normal cecum and ascending colon. The ischemic bowel is derived from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Midgut","B":"Foregut","C":"Pharyngeal gut","D":"Hindgut","E":"Cloaca"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hindgut extends from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the cloacal membrane and forms the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper anal canal, all supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery; hypoperfusion of that territory explains the pattern. The midgut forms the cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, which are normal. The foregut forms the esophagus through the proximal duodenum. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that the urorectal septum divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus; it does not form the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut = IMA, with parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves rather than the vagus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the splenic flexure is a watershed between SMA and IMA territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerves rather than the vagus supply parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has endoscopic removal of an adenoma in the descending duodenum just proximal to the major duodenal papilla. Several hours later he has bleeding from the main artery supplying the resected segment. Which artery is most likely bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B":"Right gastroepiploic artery","C":"Short gastric branch of the splenic artery","D":"Ileocolic branch of the SMA","E":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major papilla is foregut and is supplied by the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which arises from the gastroduodenal artery of the celiac system. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is an SMA branch that supplies the midgut part distal to the papilla. The right gastroepiploic artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach. Short gastric branches of the splenic artery supply the gastric fundus. The ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery, the parent of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: localize duodenal bleeding by its position relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios drools, chokes, and becomes cyanotic during his first feed. A nasogastric tube coils in the upper chest, and a chest radiograph shows a gas-filled stomach. Which additional finding is most likely in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle","B":"Annular pancreatic tissue","C":"Vertebral segmentation defects","D":"Aganglionic distal colon","E":"Bilateral congenital cataracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A blind upper esophageal pouch that stops the nasogastric tube, together with air in the stomach, indicates esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. Thirty to fifty percent of these infants have other anomalies, most notably the VACTERL association, which includes vertebral defects. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle presents weeks later with projectile nonbilious vomiting. Annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 and duodenal obstruction. Aganglionic colon is Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed meconium passage. Congenital cataracts point to infections such as rubella. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features and associated anomalies); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VACTERL = vertebral, anal atresia, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, renal, limb defects; failure to pass an NG tube is the bedside test for EA (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: screen with echocardiography and renal ultrasound once EA is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is another foregut anomaly with polyhydramnios and neonatal vomiting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-month-old girl has had three episodes of pneumonia and coughs and gags during most feeds. A contrast study shows a continuous, patent esophagus with contrast entering the trachea through a short oblique tract in the lower neck. Which developmental error best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Failed lengthening of the esophagus during descent","B":"Incomplete recanalization of the esophageal lumen","C":"Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","D":"Defective fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","E":"Incomplete fusion of tracheoesophageal ridges"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A patent esophagus with a fistula to the trachea is the H-type fistula. It results from incomplete partitioning by the tracheoesophageal septum, which forms when the paired tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse; where fusion is incomplete, a communication persists. Failed lengthening of the esophagus causes congenital hiatal hernia. Incomplete recanalization narrows the lumen as esophageal stenosis without a fistula. Loss of myenteric ganglion cells causes motility disorders such as achalasia. Defective pleuroperitoneal fold fusion causes diaphragmatic hernia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the septum and H-type TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophagus and esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H-type fistula resembles the letter H and causes recurrent aspiration pneumonia (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: suspect it in an infant with recurrent pneumonia and choking with feeds despite a passable NG tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal stenosis and webs arise from structural narrowing rather than a fistula","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks of gestation has a fundal height larger than expected. Ultrasound shows an amniotic fluid index of 32 cm (reference range: 5–25 cm) and a fetal stomach that is not visualized on repeated scans. Which finding is most likely at birth?","choices":{"A":"Failure to pass a nasogastric tube","B":"Bilious vomiting after the first feed","C":"Olive-shaped epigastric mass","D":"Delayed passage of meconium","E":"Bowel loops outside the abdomen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Polyhydramnios with an absent fetal stomach bubble means swallowed amniotic fluid is not reaching the stomach, which indicates esophageal atresia; at birth a nasogastric tube will stop in the blind upper pouch. Bilious vomiting with polyhydramnios fits duodenal atresia distal to the papilla, but that lesion produces a distended stomach and duodenum on ultrasound. The olive-shaped mass of pyloric stenosis appears weeks after birth. Delayed meconium passage suggests Hirschsprung disease or meconium ileus. Bowel loops outside the abdomen fit gastroschisis or omphalocele, which are seen directly on ultrasound. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, in utero polyhydramnios and postnatal presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia and polyhydramnios); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure EA gives a gasless abdomen, whereas EA with distal TEF gives a prominent gastric bubble (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a small or absent fetal stomach with polyhydramnios is the prenatal clue to EA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal atresia as the other foregut cause of polyhydramnios (double bubble)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with refractory peptic ulcer disease undergoes truncal vagotomy. The surgeon divides the vagal trunk that lies on the anterior surface of the distal esophagus. The fibers in this trunk come mainly from which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Right vagus nerve","B":"Left phrenic nerve","C":"Left vagus nerve","D":"Greater splanchnic nerve","E":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Before rotation the left and right vagus nerves run along the left and right sides of the stomach. The 90-degree clockwise rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly, where it becomes the anterior vagal trunk innervating the anterior gastric wall. The right vagus becomes the posterior trunk. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch and supplies the larynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, vagal innervation changes); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotation and vagus nerves).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after the 90-degree clockwise rotation the left vagus lies anterior and the right vagus posterior (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: vagotomy reduces acid secretion but can delay gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the vagus distributes parasympathetic fibers to the stomach and proximal GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man recovering from acute pancreatitis develops a fluid collection that fills the space directly behind the stomach. This space formed as a result of which effect of gastric rotation?","choices":{"A":"Leftward pull on the ventral mesogastrium","B":"Leftward pull on the dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Rightward swing of the duodenal loop","D":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","E":"Fusion of the transverse mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The space behind the stomach is the omental bursa, or lesser peritoneal sac. As the stomach rotates around its longitudinal axis, it pulls the dorsal mesogastrium to the left and opens this space behind it. The ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the lesser omentum, is pulled to the right rather than the left. The rightward swing of the C-shaped duodenum moves the duodenum and pancreatic head against the posterior wall. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the SMA positions the intestines. Fusion of the greater omentum with the transverse mesocolon occurs later and closes off the lower part of the bursa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, formation of the omental bursa); Langman Ch. 15 (omental bursa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lesser sac communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: pancreatic pseudocysts and posterior gastric ulcer perforations collect in the lesser sac.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — pancreatic pseudocysts are nonneoplastic collections that follow pancreatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3D model of a 6-week embryo shows the stomach after its first rotation has turned the original left side to face anteriorly. In a second movement, the caudal pyloric end shifts upward and to the right while the cardiac end moves slightly downward and to the left, giving the adult stomach its oblique long axis. Rotation around which axis produces this second movement?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal axis","B":"Superior mesenteric artery axis","C":"Transverse left-right axis","D":"Anteroposterior axis","E":"Vitelline duct axis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After the 90-degree clockwise turn around its longitudinal axis, the stomach rotates around an anteroposterior axis: the caudal pyloric part moves upward and to the right and the cephalic cardiac part moves slightly downward and to the left, so the adult long axis runs from upper left to lower right. The longitudinal-axis rotation is the earlier turn that brings the original left side forward and carries the left vagus anteriorly. The superior mesenteric artery axis is the axis of midgut loop rotation, not stomach rotation. A transverse left-right axis and a vitelline duct axis are not described for the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the anteroposterior axis, p5); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotates around a longitudinal and an anteroposterior axis, Fig. 15.8).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates around two axes, longitudinal then anteroposterior, which sets the positions of the pylorus and cardia (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dual rotation places the stomach in its adult position with the long axis running from upper left to lower right (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — longitudinal rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl with upslanting palpebral fissures and a single palmar crease vomits bile after every feed. An abdominal radiograph shows a dilated stomach and proximal duodenum with no gas beyond them. At surgery the pancreas is normal, and a thin membrane occludes the bowel just distal to the major papilla. Which developmental process failed?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","B":"Perfusion of the jejunal mesentery","C":"Rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud","D":"Rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The double bubble with no distal gas, bilious vomiting, and features of Down syndrome indicate duodenal atresia; an occluding membrane distal to the papilla is the result of failed vacuolization and recanalization after the solid stage. Loss of mesenteric perfusion causes jejunal atresia, with a triple bubble, and ileal atresia, with multiple dilated loops. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas, excluded by the normal pancreas at surgery. Malrotation of the midgut causes obstruction by bands or volvulus rather than an intraluminal membrane. Failed pyloric relaxation causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis with nonbilious vomiting weeks after birth. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failed recanalization with an occluding membrane, the 80–85% of obstructions distal to the papilla, and associated conditions); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (triple bubble in jejunal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = double bubble, polyhydramnios, bilious vomiting, Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: look for associated cardiac defects in infants with trisomy 21.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is the other congenital cause of a double bubble","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds. An abdominal radiograph shows a distended stomach and duodenal bulb with a small amount of gas in the distal bowel, and a contrast study shows a narrowed duodenal segment with a perforated web. Where is this lesion most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Distal to the major papilla","B":"At the duodenojejunal flexure","C":"In the terminal ileum","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Proximal to the major papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A perforated web with some distal gas indicates duodenal stenosis from incomplete recanalization, and nonbilious vomiting places the narrowing proximal to the major papilla, where bile has not yet entered the lumen. A lesion distal to the papilla or at the duodenojejunal flexure would produce bilious vomiting. Terminal ileal obstruction causes bilious vomiting with diffuse distension rather than a dilated duodenal bulb. Obstruction at the gastroesophageal junction prevents feeds from reaching the stomach, so the stomach and duodenum would not distend. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, duodenal stenosis and bilious versus nonbilious vomiting); Langman Ch. 15 (atresias and stenoses of the duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: complete atresia gives no distal gas; stenosis or a perforated web lets some gas pass (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bile in the vomitus localizes the obstruction relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the bile duct enters at the major papilla as the duodenum and ventral bud rotate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman at 24 weeks of gestation has an ultrasound showing polyhydramnios and two fluid-filled structures in the fetal upper abdomen that connect through the pylorus. No other fluid-filled bowel is seen. The fetus is most likely to have which additional condition?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Trisomy 21","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Turner syndrome","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A fluid-filled stomach and proximal duodenum connected through the pylorus is the prenatal double bubble of duodenal atresia, and polyhydramnios reflects swallowed fluid that does not reach the distal bowel. Duodenal atresia is strongly associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21), present in up to 30% of cases. Trisomy 18 and Turner syndrome carry other congenital anomalies but are not the classic association. Trisomy 13 is associated with midline defects such as holoprosencephaly. Klinefelter syndrome has no characteristic GI atresia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, associated conditions); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenal atresia); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Down syndrome GI associations include duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a prenatal double bubble prompts karyotype counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old firstborn boy has had projectile, nonbilious vomiting after every feed for 5 days and is eager to feed again right after vomiting. A firm, mobile mass is palpable in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 88 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 33 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The palpable mass consists mainly of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Edematous submucosal connective tissue","B":"Hyperplastic antral mucosal glands","C":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle","D":"Enlarged submucosal lymphoid follicles","E":"Heterotopic pancreatic tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hungry, firstborn male infant with projectile nonbilious vomiting, an epigastric olive, and hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis has hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The olive is the hypertrophied and hyperplastic circular smooth muscle of the pyloric muscularis propria. Mucosal and submucosal edema are secondary findings and do not form the mass. Antral gland hyperplasia and lymphoid follicles are not part of the lesion; lymphoid hyperplasia is a lead point for intussusception. Heterotopic pancreatic tissue can form a small gastric nodule but does not cause this presentation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, developmental abnormality and clinical presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis = olive mass, visible peristalsis, projectile nonbilious vomiting at about 3–6 weeks (lecture; First Aid gives 2–6 weeks), hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: correct the fluid and electrolyte deficit before pyloromyotomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — loss of parietal cell HCl and the alkaline tide explain the metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl whose mother has pertussis was given a 5-day course of a prophylactic antibiotic starting at 6 days of age. She now has projectile, nonbilious vomiting after feeds and an olive-shaped epigastric mass. The prophylactic drug most likely acts by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit","B":"Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit","C":"Inhibiting cell wall transpeptidase","D":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase","E":"Inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pertussis prophylaxis in a young infant uses a macrolide, and macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks of life is associated with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Macrolides such as erythromycin and azithromycin bind the 50S ribosomal subunit and block translocation. Binding the 30S subunit is the mechanism of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidases is the mechanism of beta-lactams. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones. Inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase is the mechanism of sulfonamides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks); Langman Ch. 15 (erythromycin in the newborn period); First Aid 2025 GI (macrolide exposure); Katzung Ch. 44 (macrolides bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, Fig. 44–1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI); erythromycin also acts as a motilin agonist and prokinetic. COMLEX: counsel families about vomiting after neonatal macrolide therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal feedback and pyloric resistance control gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old girl has forceful vomiting immediately after feeds and remains hungry afterward. Ultrasound shows a thickened, elongated pyloric channel, and her vomitus contains milk but no bile. Which of the following best explains the absence of bile in her vomitus?","choices":{"A":"Absent bile secretion before 3 months of age","B":"Reflux blocked by the sphincter of Oddi","C":"Bile reabsorbed in the gastric antrum","D":"Obstruction proximal to the major papilla","E":"Bile diverted through the cystic duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pylorus lies proximal to the major duodenal papilla, where bile enters the gut, so a pyloric obstruction keeps bile out of the stomach and the vomitus is nonbilious. Infants secrete bile from birth, so age does not explain it. The sphincter of Oddi regulates bile entry into the duodenum but does not prevent bile from reaching the stomach when the obstruction lies distal to the papilla. The gastric antrum does not reabsorb bile; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Diversion through the cystic duct stores bile in the gallbladder but does not eliminate it from the duodenum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, forceful projectile nonbilious vomiting); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonbilious projectile vomiting points to pyloric stenosis; bilious vomiting points to obstruction distal to the ampulla (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an ultrasound of the pylorus confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — bile enters the duodenum through the sphincter of Oddi under CCK control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT shows an embolus in the artery that leaves the aorta just below the celiac trunk, with ischemia from the jejunum to the proximal transverse colon. The ischemic bowel comes from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Foregut","B":"Hindgut","C":"Midgut","D":"Cloaca","E":"Pharyngeal gut"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The artery just below the celiac trunk is the superior mesenteric artery, the artery of the midgut, which forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The foregut is supplied by the celiac artery. The hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon onward, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The cloaca is the terminal hindgut that forms the rectum and urogenital sinus. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has a congenital tracheal anomaly. His airway primordium first appeared at about 4 weeks of gestation as an outgrowth of which region of the gut tube?","choices":{"A":"Ventral foregut wall","B":"Dorsal foregut wall","C":"Ventral midgut wall","D":"Dorsal pharyngeal wall","E":"Lateral hindgut wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At about 4 weeks, the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) appears on the ventral wall of the foregut at its border with the pharyngeal gut; the ventral part of the partitioned foregut forms the trachea and lung buds, and the dorsal part forms the esophagus. The dorsal foregut forms the esophagus rather than the airway. The midgut, the dorsal pharyngeal wall, and the hindgut do not give rise to the respiratory primordium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the tracheoesophageal septum); Langman Ch. 15 (respiratory diverticulum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the respiratory diverticulum forms at about 4 weeks of gestation (lecture SDL 1). COMLEX: the foregut also gives rise to the stomach and proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelium of the esophagus and airway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has a splenectomy after a motor vehicle collision. While dividing the peritoneal fold that runs from the spleen to the left kidney, the surgeon protects a structure that lies within it. Which structure does this ligament contain?","choices":{"A":"Short gastric arteries","B":"Left gastric artery","C":"Left adrenal gland","D":"Tail of the pancreas","E":"Left gonadal vein"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The part of the dorsal mesogastrium between the spleen and the left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament, and the tail of the pancreas, which grows from the dorsal mesoduodenum into the dorsal mesogastrium, lies in it along with the splenic artery and vein. The short gastric arteries run in the gastrosplenic ligament. The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature in the lesser omentum. The left adrenal gland and left gonadal vein are retroperitoneal structures behind the peritoneum, not within the ligament. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen and pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (ligaments: splenorenal ligament contains the splenic vessels and tail of pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas, and the gastrosplenic ligament contains the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments). COMLEX: the pancreatic tail can be injured during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — pancreatic buds in the dorsal and ventral mesentery","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman brings her 3-month-old son for follow-up after repair of a duodenal stenosis caused by a perforated mucosal web. He has no features of a chromosomal syndrome and is now feeding well. She asks when in development the lesion began. The surgeon explains that it arose from a stage when proliferating epithelium temporarily filled the duodenal lumen. During which period of gestation did that stage occur?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 6","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 8 to 10","D":"Weeks 12 to 14","E":"Weeks 20 to 24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A perforated duodenal web causes stenosis through incomplete recanalization of the solid epithelial cord. During weeks 5 to 6, rapid proliferation of the endodermal epithelium fills the duodenal lumen and forms the solid stage; vacuolization and coalescence of vacuoles then restore the lumen. Weeks 2 to 3 precede folding of the embryo into a gut tube, so there is no duodenal lumen to fill. By weeks 8 to 10 the lumen is normally fully restored, which marks the end of recanalization rather than the solid stage. Weeks 12 to 14 and weeks 20 to 24 come after the lumen has already reopened. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, solid stage at weeks 5 to 6 and lumen restoration by weeks 8 to 10, lines 189–203, and duodenal stenosis from a perforated web, lines 210–212); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal atresia, failed recanalization).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists VACTERL syndrome and trisomy 21 as associations of duodenal atresia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, duodenal atresia). COMLEX: First Aid notes that intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension within the first 1–2 days of life (First Aid 2025 GI, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy whose father had a pyloromyotomy as an infant has had forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds for 4 days and cries to be fed again afterward. He appears mildly dehydrated. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 91 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L) and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Inspection of his abdomen just before an episode of emesis is most likely to show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Waves moving right to left across the lower abdomen","B":"Waves moving left to right across the epigastrium","C":"A sausage shaped mass in the right upper quadrant","D":"Diffuse distension of the lower abdomen","E":"Bluish periumbilical discoloration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds in a hungry young infant with a family history of the disorder and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis indicates hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. As the hypertrophied antrum tries to drive chyme past the narrowed pylorus, prominent left-to-right peristaltic waves can be seen crossing the epigastrium just before emesis. Waves running right to left over the lower abdomen do not follow gastric peristalsis toward the pylorus. A sausage shaped right upper quadrant mass suggests intussusception, which causes colicky pain and bloody stools rather than projectile vomiting in a hungry infant. Diffuse lower abdominal distension points to a distal bowel obstruction, whereas pyloric obstruction leaves the lower abdomen flat. Bluish periumbilical discoloration reflects retroperitoneal hemorrhage, as in hemorrhagic pancreatitis, not gastric outlet obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, family history, hungry vomiter, visible gastric peristalsis, and metabolic alkalosis, lines 268–293); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peristaltic wave in epigastrium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists bottle feeding, nicotine exposure, erythromycin, and azithromycin as risk factors for pyloric stenosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis). COMLEX: First Aid notes that ultrasound shows a thickened and lengthened pylorus (First Aid 2025 GI, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — pyloric resistance in gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stool after a period of hypotension during cardiac surgery. Colonoscopy shows ischemia centered on the splenic flexure. Why is this site vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Celiac and SMA boundary","B":"Portal and caval anastomosis","C":"Hindgut and cloaca junction","D":"Single IMA end artery","E":"SMA and IMA watershed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies near the boundary between the midgut territory of the superior mesenteric artery and the hindgut territory of the inferior mesenteric artery, a watershed area that is vulnerable to low flow. The celiac and SMA territories meet at the duodenum. A portal and caval anastomosis is a venous connection in the esophagus, rectum, or umbilical region and does not explain arterial low flow ischemia. The hindgut and cloaca junction is in the pelvis. A single end artery does not describe the flexure, whose supply comes from the edges of two territories, which is what makes it a watershed. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, midgut and hindgut boundary at the distal third of the transverse colon); First Aid 2025 GI (colonic ischemia at watershed areas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic colitis affects watershed areas such as the splenic flexure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: pain followed by bloody diarrhea after hypotension suggests colonic ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the midgut–hindgut boundary near the splenic flexure, where vagal and pelvic parasympathetic territories also meet","isHighYield":true}],"2":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between which two parts of the alimentary canal wall?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium and lamina propria","B":"Inner and outer muscle layers","C":"Submucosa and serosa","D":"Mucosa and submucosa","E":"Lamina propria and serosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the mucosa, a thin band of smooth muscle that separates the mucosa from the submucosa beneath it. The epithelium and lamina propria are both above it, inside the mucosa. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers belong to the muscularis externa and are separated by the myenteric plexus. The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae; the submucosa and muscularis externa lie between them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, mucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between mucosa and submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers from inside out are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (MSMS); erosions stay in the mucosa, while ulcers extend into the submucosa or deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: depth of a lesion relative to the muscularis mucosae separates an erosion from an ulcer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the thoracic esophagus is fixed to surrounding mediastinal structures for most of its length. Which coat forms its outermost layer?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Mesentery","D":"Submucosa","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Where the gut tube is bound to adjacent structures, its outer coat is adventitia, connective tissue that blends with the surrounding connective tissue; the thoracic esophagus is the classic example. Serosa, a simple squamous mesothelium on loose connective tissue, covers only the short abdominal segment after the esophagus enters the peritoneal cavity. Mesothelium is the epithelial part of a serosa, so it is absent where there is no serosa. A mesentery suspends intraperitoneal bowel and does not cover the thoracic esophagus. The submucosa lies inside the muscularis externa, not outside it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa or adventitia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal outer layer is adventitia; the abdominal segment is covered by serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serosa covers intraperitoneal segments and adventitia covers retroperitoneal or fixed segments (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the thoracic esophagus lacks a serosal covering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa of the small intestine, contraction of which component shortens the gut tube during peristalsis?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Outer longitudinal layer","D":"Inner oblique layer","E":"Lamina propria myofibroblasts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis externa has an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the tube, while contraction of the inner circular layer constricts the lumen; together they drive peristalsis and segmentation. The muscularis mucosae lies in the mucosa and makes local folds without moving the whole segment. An inner oblique layer is a regional feature of the stomach, not the small intestine. Myofibroblasts in the lamina propria do not form a muscle layer that shortens the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two layers of the muscularis externa controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the stomach adds an inner oblique layer and the colon condenses its longitudinal layer into teniae coli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which epithelium lines the human esophagus and resists abrasion by swallowed food?","choices":{"A":"Keratinized stratified squamous","B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","C":"Simple columnar with goblet cells","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","E":"Stratified columnar with microvilli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose multiple cell layers resist abrasion from rough food boluses while staying moist. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is not the normal human esophageal lining. Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is the intestinal pattern and appears in the esophagus only as Barrett metaplasia. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airway. Stratified columnar epithelium with microvilli is not a lining of the alimentary canal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; Barrett esophagus replaces it with intestinal columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology and Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic acid reflux drives this metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"The surface mucous cells of the stomach secrete a thick gel that protects the epithelium from luminal acid. This gel is rich in which ions?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen and chloride","B":"Ammonium and phosphate","C":"Calcium and magnesium","D":"Bicarbonate and K+","E":"Sodium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surface mucous cells secrete a viscous, visible mucus layer rich in bicarbonate and K+. The bicarbonate trapped in the gel neutralizes acid at the epithelial surface, and the gel slows pepsin, which protects the mucosa from autodigestion. Hydrogen and chloride ions are the components of the hydrochloric acid secreted by parietal cells, the injury the gel guards against. Ammonium is generated by H. pylori urease, not by surface mucous cells. Phosphate, calcium, magnesium, and sulfate are not the defining ions of this barrier. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins maintain gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion; NSAIDs reduce them and predispose to ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol, a prostaglandin analog, restores this protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which crypt-base cell type of the small intestine secretes lysozyme and alpha-defensins?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Enteroendocrine cells","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Enterocytes","E":"Tuft cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete lysozyme and alpha-defensins, antimicrobial products of innate immunity. Goblet cells secrete mucin for lubrication. Enteroendocrine cells release peptide hormones such as CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin. Enterocytes absorb nutrients through their microvillous brush border. Tuft cells are chemosensory cells and do not secrete these antimicrobial proteins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, secretion in the small intestine); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (Paneth cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells and Paneth cells that secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: Paneth cells sit at the crypt base, below the stem cells that renew the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, where in the wall of the alimentary canal is the myenteric plexus located?","choices":{"A":"Between the muscle layers","B":"Within the submucosa","C":"Within the lamina propria","D":"Beneath the epithelium","E":"Within the adventitia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, where it innervates both layers. The submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The lamina propria and the region beneath the epithelium carry nerve fibers but not the myenteric ganglia. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat outside the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, anatomic location); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (myenteric plexus between the outer and inner muscle layers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosa contains the Meissner plexus; muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease shows absent ganglion cells in both plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"In the enteric nervous system, which plexus mainly regulates local secretion, absorption, and blood flow in each gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Hypogastric plexus","D":"Esophageal vagal plexus","E":"Submucosal plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus innervates mucosal and submucosal glands, local blood vessels, and the muscularis mucosae, controlling secretion, absorption, blood flow, and mucosal folding in each segment. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus controls motility of the muscularis externa along the length of the gut. The celiac and hypogastric plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. The esophageal vagal plexus carries extrinsic vagal fibers on the esophagus surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, main functional role); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosal plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meissner plexus regulates secretion and blood flow; Auerbach plexus regulates motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: both plexuses receive extrinsic vagal and sympathetic modulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"Inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus relax the lower esophageal sphincter to let a bolus enter the stomach. Which transmitter do these neurons release?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Nitric oxide","C":"Substance P","D":"Norepinephrine","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibitory peptidergic neurons of the myenteric plexus release nitric oxide, together with VIP or ATP, to relax GI sphincters such as the lower esophageal, pyloric, and ileocecal sphincters. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Norepinephrine comes from extrinsic sympathetic postganglionic fibers and inhibits motility rather than mediating enteric sphincter relaxation. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and paracrine signal in the mucosa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia is caused by loss of NO and VIP inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: bird beak narrowing on barium swallow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis externa of the upper third of the esophagus consists of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Smooth muscle","C":"Mixed striated and smooth","D":"Cardiac muscle","E":"Myoepithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is striated muscle, a continuation of the pharyngeal musculature. Smooth muscle forms the distal third. Mixed and interwoven striated and smooth bundles form the middle third. Cardiac muscle is not part of the gut wall. Myoepithelial cells surround the secretory units of some glands and do not form the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (upper one-third is striated muscle).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 of the esophagus is striated muscle, middle 1/3 mixed, and lower 1/3 smooth muscle (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI groups the middle and lower 2/3 as smooth muscle with some overlap at the transition). COMLEX: upper esophageal dysphagia suggests a skeletal muscle or neuromuscular cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"The striated muscle of the proximal esophagus is supplied by somatic motor neurons that travel in the vagus nerve. From which brainstem nucleus do these neurons arise?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Nucleus ambiguus","E":"Facial motor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Somatic motor neurons to the striated esophageal muscle arise in the nucleus ambiguus and end directly on motor endplates. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. The hypoglossal nucleus supplies tongue muscles. The nucleus tractus solitarius receives visceral afferent input. The facial motor nucleus supplies muscles of facial expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus travel in the vagus to the striated muscle of the upper esophagus, whereas the dorsal motor nucleus supplies the smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: a brainstem lesion involving the nucleus ambiguus can cause dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus reach the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Where do these fibers synapse?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion neurons","B":"Motor endplates on muscle","C":"Myenteric plexus neurons","D":"Stellate ganglion neurons","E":"Submucosal gland cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal preganglionic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse on postganglionic enteric neurons whose cell bodies lie in the myenteric plexus of the esophageal wall; these neurons then drive contraction and peristalsis. The celiac and stellate ganglia are sympathetic ganglia. Motor endplates are the targets of somatic fibers from the nucleus ambiguus on striated muscle, not of preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. Submucosal gland cells are effectors, not the site of the ganglionic synapse. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, parasympathetic control of smooth musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long and synapse in ganglia in or near the target organ, within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal input enhances peristalsis through the enteric neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper are located in which layer of the esophageal wall?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria","B":"Adventitia","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Esophageal glands proper are small compound tubuloalveolar glands in the submucosa, and their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and mucosa to open into the lumen. The lamina propria holds the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat. The epithelium is crossed only by the ducts. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer that the ducts pass through. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands are found in the esophagus and in the duodenum as Brunner glands (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: identify gland layer to name the gland type on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction secrete which product?","choices":{"A":"Neutral mucus","B":"Acidic mucus","C":"Serous fluid","D":"Bicarbonate fluid","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Esophageal cardiac glands secrete neutral mucus that protects the distal esophagus from regurgitated gastric contents. The esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic mucus, which makes acidic mucus the wrong gland. Serous fluid comes from serous glands such as the parotid. Bicarbonate fluid is the product of duodenal Brunner glands. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands produce neutral mucus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when reflux overwhelms distal protection, GERD can lead to Barrett metaplasia (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin injury of the distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic, viscous mucus. What is the main function of this secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the lumen","C":"Digesting dietary starch","D":"Absorbing swallowed water","E":"Secreting immunoglobulin A"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The slightly acidic mucus of the esophageal glands proper lubricates the luminal surface so boluses pass smoothly and the squamous lining is protected from mechanical injury. Neutralizing refluxed acid is the role of the neutral mucus from esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach. Starch digestion begins with salivary amylase, not esophageal mucus. The esophagus does not absorb swallowed water. IgA is secreted by plasma cells and transported across epithelium, not produced by these glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, secretion type and function); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mucus of glands proper lubricates the luminal wall).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal mucosa is protective, not absorptive (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: impaired lubrication contributes to the sensation of food sticking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman who takes daily naproxen for arthritis has epigastric pain. Endoscopic biopsy of a gastric lesion shows that the tissue loss has crossed the muscularis mucosae and ends in dense irregular connective tissue containing large vessels. Which layer forms the base of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Adventitia","D":"Mesothelium","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large blood and lymphatic vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa. A defect that reaches it is an ulcer rather than an erosion. The epithelium and lamina propria lie above the muscularis mucosae, so a lesion ending there would be an erosion. The adventitia and the mesothelium of the serosa lie outside the muscularis externa, much deeper than this lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, submucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are confined to the mucosa; ulcers extend into the submucosa or muscularis (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers by reducing prostaglandins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has squamous cell carcinoma of the mid-thoracic esophagus. CT shows the tumor extending directly into the surrounding mediastinal fat. Absence of which layer in this segment of the esophageal wall helps explain this spread?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mucosa","C":"Submucosa","D":"Adventitia","E":"Epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thoracic esophagus has no serosa; its outer coat is adventitia, which blends into the mediastinal connective tissue, so there is no mesothelial boundary between the tumor and the mediastinum. Serosa covers only the short abdominal segment. The mucosa, submucosa, and epithelium are present in the thoracic esophagus, and the tumor arises from its epithelium. Adventitia is present, not absent, and it is the coat the tumor crosses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, adventitia of the thoracic esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal adventitia); First Aid 2025 GI (esophageal cancer: lack of serosa in the esophageal wall allows rapid extension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma involves the upper 2/3 of the esophagus and adenocarcinoma the lower 1/3 (First Aid 2025 GI esophageal cancer). COMLEX: smoking and alcohol are leading risks for squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — anatomic distribution of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes small-bowel biopsy for bloating. The pathologist points to a thin band of smooth muscle at the base of the crypts that separates the mucosa from the submucosa. Contraction of this band has which effect?","choices":{"A":"Propulsive peristalsis of chyme","B":"Shortening of the bowel segment","C":"Local ridges in the inner lining","D":"Closure of the ileocecal valve","E":"Constriction of the full bowel lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The band is the muscularis mucosae. Its local contractions raise folds and ridges of the mucosa that improve contact with chyme for absorption and secretion, independent of whole-organ peristalsis. Propulsive peristalsis, shortening of the segment, and constriction of the whole lumen come from the outer longitudinal and inner circular layers of the muscularis externa. Closure of the ileocecal valve depends on sphincteric circular muscle of the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis mucosae); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae, local secretion, and blood flow (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: mucosal movement aids contact between chyme and the absorptive surface.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows flattened mucosa with loss of the finger-like projections that contain central lacteals. Loss of these projections impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Acid neutralization","B":"Pathogen sampling","C":"Peristaltic propulsion","D":"Nutrient absorption","E":"Pepsinogen secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The finger-like projections are villi, cores of lamina propria with capillaries and a central lacteal covered by enterocytes. With plicae circulares and microvilli, they amplify absorptive surface area, so villous atrophy impairs nutrient absorption. Acid neutralization in the duodenum depends on Brunner gland and pancreatic bicarbonate. Pathogen sampling is the role of Peyer patches and M cells. Peristaltic propulsion depends on the muscularis externa. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption and amplification specializations); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (villi).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis, mostly in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI celiac disease). COMLEX: iron deficiency reflects duodenal malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — loss of specialized epithelial cells causing malabsorption, as parietal-cell loss impairs B12 absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with long-standing heartburn undergoes endoscopy. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal lining. The replaced lining was specialized for which function?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing nutrients and water","B":"Secreting acid and pepsinogen","C":"Resisting mechanical abrasion","D":"Sampling luminal antigens","E":"Secreting antimicrobial peptides"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose layered cells resist mechanical abrasion from food boluses. Here it has been replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium, which is Barrett esophagus. Absorbing nutrients and water is the role of intestinal enterocytes. Secreting acid and pepsinogen is the role of gastric parietal and chief cells. Sampling luminal antigens is done by M cells over Peyer patches. Secreting antimicrobial peptides is the role of Paneth cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection, and section 5, cardiac glands and Barrett esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal epithelium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the distal esophagus, a precursor of adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic GERD is the driving exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — GERD and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman who takes high-dose aspirin develops gastric erosions. The drug reduces prostaglandin-driven secretion by the cells that line the gastric surface and pits. Loss of their secretion impairs which protective mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Killing microbes with lysozyme","B":"Neutralizing acid at the epithelium","C":"Resisting abrasion with keratin","D":"Absorbing acid through tight junctions","E":"Trapping antigens in Peyer patches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surface mucous cells line the gastric surface and pits and secrete a viscous mucus rich in bicarbonate that neutralizes acid at the epithelium and slows pepsin. Prostaglandins sustain this secretion, so aspirin weakens the barrier. Killing microbes with lysozyme is a Paneth cell function in the intestine. Gastric epithelium is columnar and not keratinized. Tight junctions seal the epithelium against back-diffusion of acid rather than absorbing it. Peyer patches are lymphoid aggregates of the ileum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs inhibit COX and reduce prostaglandin-dependent mucus and bicarbonate secretion, causing erosions and ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol is used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distension. Full-thickness rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers or in the submucosa. Loss of the ganglia between the muscle layers impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Local secretion by mucosal glands","B":"Regulation of submucosal blood flow","C":"Coordinated propulsive motility","D":"Absorption of nutrients by enterocytes","E":"Antigen sampling by Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the circular and longitudinal layers form the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls tone, rhythmic contraction, and conduction of peristaltic waves and relaxes sphincters. Without it the aganglionic segment stays contracted and does not propel stool. Local secretion and submucosal blood flow are mainly regulated by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. Absorption by enterocytes is an epithelial function. Antigen sampling is a lymphoid function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus function); First Aid 2025 GI (Hirschsprung disease; suction biopsy, which samples mucosa and submucosa, is the usual first test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease is absent ganglion cells in both plexuses of the distal colon from failed neural crest migration; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy; associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: empty rectum with explosive stool on rectal exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes part in a study of a toxin that selectively destroys the enteric motor neurons, lying between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers, that relax gut smooth muscle. Failed opening of which of the following would be expected?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Upper esophageal sphincter","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Puborectalis sling","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptidergic relaxing neurons of the Auerbach plexus open the smooth muscle sphincters of the gut, including the pyloric and ileocecal sphincters, so their loss leaves the ileocecal sphincter contracted; their transmitters are nitric oxide, VIP, and ATP. The external anal sphincter and the puborectalis sling are skeletal muscle under somatic control. The upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle driven by vagal motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus. The diaphragmatic crura are skeletal muscle that reinforce the lower esophageal sphincter from outside. The submucosal plexus has only a minimal role in sphincter tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control by the myenteric plexus, p13; section 4, striated muscle innervation, p14); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myenteric inhibitory neurons that release NO and VIP relax the LES, pylorus, and ileocecal sphincter; achalasia is loss of these neurons in the distal esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls secretion and blood flow and has little effect on sphincter tone (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — inhibitory enteric neurons release VIP, nitric oxide, and ATP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man receives a small-bowel transplant, which severs all extrinsic vagal and sympathetic nerves to the graft. Within days the graft shows coordinated peristalsis and local secretion. Which explains this function?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic enteric plexuses remain","B":"Sympathetic fibers regenerate","C":"Vagal fibers regrow into the graft","D":"Smooth muscle needs no neurons","E":"Hormones replace neural control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The enteric nervous system is an intrinsic network of neurons and glia in two plexuses inside the gut wall. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis and the submucosal plexus controls local secretion, and both work without extrinsic input, which only modulates them. Severed sympathetic and vagal fibers do not regenerate into a graft within days. Smooth muscle pacing alone does not coordinate peristalsis. Hormones modulate gut function but do not replace neural reflex circuits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation, and section 4, intrinsic autonomy); Guyton Ch. 63 (enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system can function independently of extrinsic nerves, which modulate it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic input increases and sympathetic input decreases peristalsis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — extrinsic modulation of the enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has a brainstem stroke with hoarseness and difficulty initiating swallowing. Manometry shows weak contractions in the upper esophagus with normal peristalsis in the distal esophagus. Which tissue in the upper esophagus explains this selective weakness?","choices":{"A":"Visceral smooth muscle","B":"Abundant myenteric neurons","C":"Serosal covering","D":"Striated skeletal muscle","E":"Cardiac gland mucosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The upper third of the esophagus is striated skeletal muscle supplied by somatic motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus, which a brainstem stroke can damage. The distal esophagus is smooth muscle driven by the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and the myenteric plexus, which can keep running on its own, so distal peristalsis is preserved. Visceral smooth muscle is the distal tissue, the one spared. Myenteric neurons are not selectively abundant in the upper third. The thoracic esophagus has adventitia, not serosa. Cardiac glands are mucosal glands and do not contract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions and innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 striated, middle 1/3 mixed, lower 1/3 smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its brainstem control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — neural coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon and sclerodactyly has heartburn and dysphagia. Manometry shows absent contractions in the distal esophagus with low lower sphincter pressure and normal proximal contractions. Which tissue is affected?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Pharyngeal constrictors","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Cricopharyngeus","E":"Smooth muscle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Scleroderma causes atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle, which makes up the distal esophagus and the lower sphincter, so distal peristalsis and sphincter tone fail. Striated muscle of the proximal third is spared, which fits the normal proximal contractions. The pharyngeal constrictors and cricopharyngeus are striated muscles above or at the upper esophagus. The diaphragmatic crura reinforce the sphincter externally but are skeletal muscle and not the site of disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); First Aid 2025 GI (scleroderma esophageal dysmotility).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma causes esophageal smooth muscle atrophy with decreased LES pressure and distal dysmotility, leading to reflux and dysphagia; part of CREST (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: GERD from scleroderma can cause strictures and Barrett esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has injury to both vagal trunks during a thoracic operation. Afterward, balloon distension of the distal esophagus still triggers peristaltic waves in that segment. What mediates these waves?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus output","B":"Myenteric plexus reflexes","C":"Glossopharyngeal afferents","D":"Phrenic nerve reflexes","E":"Sympathetic celiac outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When vagal fibers to the distal esophagus are interrupted, the intrinsic myenteric plexus can still start secondary peristaltic waves in response to local distension. The nucleus ambiguus sends its output through the vagus, which is injured, and it supplies striated muscle. Glossopharyngeal afferents trigger the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, not local esophageal reflexes. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. Sympathetic celiac outflow suppresses esophageal motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, intrinsic autonomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and begins at the point of distension (Costanzo Ch. 8; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6, secondary peristalsis). COMLEX: intrinsic reflexes keep some motility after vagal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — primary versus secondary esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with occasional heartburn has biopsies taken just above the gastroesophageal junction. The pathologist notes branched tubular mucous glands in the lamina propria that resemble gastric cardiac glands. What is the main function of these glands?","choices":{"A":"Protecting against refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the upper esophagus","C":"Secreting acid into the lumen","D":"Secreting pepsinogen for digestion","E":"Absorbing fluid from the bolus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These are esophageal cardiac glands. Their neutral mucus protects the distal esophageal mucosa from regurgitated acidic gastric juice; when reflux overwhelms them, heartburn and GERD follow. Lubricating the upper esophagus is the role of the esophageal glands proper in the submucosa, which are concentrated in the upper half. Acid is secreted by gastric parietal cells and pepsinogen by chief cells, not by these mucous glands. The esophagus does not absorb fluid from the bolus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD complications include erosive esophagitis, strictures, and Barrett esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI GERD). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin contact with distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury of the distal esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist counts submucosal mucous glands with dilated ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium in sections taken at several levels of an autopsy esophagus. Where are these glands most densely concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment below the hiatus","B":"Upper half of the esophagus","C":"Region just above the cardia","D":"Evenly along its length","E":"Distal third of smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Submucosal tubuloalveolar glands with dilated squamous-lined ducts are esophageal glands proper. They are scattered along the whole esophagus but are most densely concentrated in its upper half, where their viscous mucus lubricates the lumen. The region just above the cardia is where esophageal cardiac glands, which lie in the lamina propria, are found. The abdominal segment and the distal smooth-muscle third are not where glands proper are densest. Their distribution is not even, because they cluster in the upper half. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper concentrated in the upper half, p15); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, comparison table, p20).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa and esophageal cardiac glands in the lamina propria (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: cardiac glands sit near the gastroesophageal junction, where their neutral mucus meets refluxed acid (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 5 — esophageal versus gastric glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endoscopy for intermittent dysphagia. Small cysts are found in the upper esophagus, and biopsy shows dilated submucosal ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium and filled with mucus. Which property of the secretion favors these cysts?","choices":{"A":"High bicarbonate content","B":"Enzymatic digestion of ducts","C":"Neutral pH of the mucus","D":"High viscosity of the mucus","E":"Serous watery composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ducts belong to esophageal glands proper, which secrete a viscous, slightly acidic mucus. Its viscosity favors transient cysts in the ducts. High bicarbonate content is typical of Brunner gland and pancreatic secretion. These glands make mucus, not enzymes that digest ducts. Neutral mucus is the product of the cardiac glands, not the glands proper. A serous watery secretion would flow freely and is not what these glands make. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (viscous secretion; transient cysts in the ducts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal epithelium is nonkeratinized stratified squamous (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: esophageal glands proper are most concentrated in the upper half of the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — structural causes of dysphagia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has free air under the diaphragm after an anterior gastric ulcer perforates through the outermost coat of the stomach wall. Which tissue lines that coat?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Transitional epithelium","D":"Dense irregular connective tissue","E":"Simple squamous mesothelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The stomach is intraperitoneal, so its outermost coat is a serosa, which consists of a simple squamous mesothelium over a thin layer of connective tissue. Simple columnar epithelium lines the gastric lumen. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus. Dense irregular connective tissue forms the submucosa and the adventitia of fixed segments. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa is a simple squamous mesothelium, lines 336–337); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcers can extend into the submucosa and muscular layers; erosions stay in the mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: perforation causes peritonitis with free air.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy develops an ileocolic intussusception a week after a viral illness. The lead point is enlarged lymphoid tissue in the wall of the distal ileum. What is this lymphoid tissue called?","choices":{"A":"Brunner glands","B":"Peyer patches","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Plicae circulares","E":"Crypts of Lieberkühn"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peyer patches are aggregated lymphatic nodules in the lamina propria and submucosa of the distal ileum, part of the gut-associated lymphatic tissue that samples and neutralizes ingested pathogens. Brunner glands are submucosal bicarbonate-secreting glands of the duodenum. Paneth cells are crypt-base cells that secrete lysozyme and defensins. Plicae circulares are transverse folds of mucosa and submucosa. Crypts of Lieberkühn are intestinal glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, immune barrier); First Aid 2025 GI (Peyer patches).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum, and their B cells make IgA (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Peyer patch hypertrophy can act as an intussusception lead point in young children (First Aid 2025 GI intussusception).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the source of gastric MALT lymphoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from rural Brazil has progressive dysphagia, and serology is positive for Trypanosoma cruzi. Biopsy shows destruction of the neurons between the muscle layers of the distal esophagus. Which function is lost?","choices":{"A":"LES relaxation","B":"Gland secretion","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Bolus lubrication","E":"Gastric acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chagas disease destroys neurons of the myenteric plexus, including the inhibitory NO and VIP neurons that relax the lower esophageal sphincter, producing secondary achalasia. Gland secretion and mucosal blood flow are mainly controlled by the submucosal plexus. Bolus lubrication comes from the esophageal glands proper. Gastric acid output is controlled at the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus and sphincter control); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia and Chagas disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary achalasia can result from Chagas disease (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: barium swallow shows bird beak narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — achalasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has an esophagectomy, and a section from the upper third shows cross-striated fibers in the muscularis externa. What innervates this muscle?","choices":{"A":"Preganglionic vagal fibers","B":"Sympathetic celiac fibers","C":"Glossopharyngeal fibers","D":"Vagal branchial efferents","E":"Phrenic motor fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Striated muscle in the upper third is supplied by motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus whose axons travel in the vagus and end on motor endplates; the lecture calls them somatic motor neurons, and standard texts classify them as branchial or special visceral efferents. Preganglionic vagal fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse in the myenteric plexus to control the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Sympathetic celiac fibers supply smooth muscle and vessels and suppress motility. Glossopharyngeal fibers serve the pharynx. Phrenic fibers supply the diaphragm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophagus is striated muscle and the lower esophagus smooth muscle (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — swallowing coordination","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":3,"stem":"A biopsy taken from the esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction shows branched, tortuous tubular mucous glands lying in the lamina propria. By which route do these glands deliver their secretion to the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Long squamous-lined ducts through the muscularis mucosae","B":"Intercalated ducts lined by low cuboidal cells","C":"Striated ducts with basal membrane infoldings","D":"Myoepithelial-wrapped ducts into the gastric cardia","E":"Short columnar-lined ducts into shallow mucosal pits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lamina propria glands near the gastroesophageal junction are esophageal cardiac glands. They are branched tubular glands of columnar mucous cells that empty through short columnar-lined duct segments into the base of shallow mucosal depressions. Long ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium that pass through the muscularis mucosae belong to the submucosal esophageal glands proper. Intercalated ducts with low cuboidal cells belong to the exocrine pancreas. Striated ducts with basal infoldings belong to the salivary glands. Ducts wrapped by myoepithelial cells that open into the gastric cardia are not described for these glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, morphology of esophageal cardiac glands, p15); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal cardiac glands resemble gastric cardiac glands and secrete neutral mucus that buffers refluxed acid (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: when reflux overwhelms this protection, GERD can drive Barrett metaplasia (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — cardiac-gland mucus in the reflux barrier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has his gallbladder removed for pigment stones from a hemolytic anemia. Which epithelium lines the mucosa of the resected organ?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar with microvilli","B":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli lines the small and large intestines and the gallbladder. Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the gallbladder lining. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oral cavity, esophagus, and lower anal canal. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airway. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption: simple columnar epithelium with microvilli). The airway and urinary linings are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: simple columnar cells of the large intestine mainly resorb water and electrolytes (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — gallbladder wall histology","isHighYield":true}],"3":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, in which region of the stomach do the gastric pits extend through about half the thickness of the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac region","B":"Fundic region","C":"Esophagogastric junction","D":"Pyloric region","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pyloric region has deep gastric pits that occupy about half of the mucosal thickness, opening onto branched, coiled pyloric glands with wide lumina. The cardiac region has relatively shallow pits over tubular mucous glands. The fundic region has shallow pits, about one-fourth to one-third of the mucosal thickness, with long fundic glands below. The esophagogastric junction is lined by squamous epithelium on its esophageal side and lacks gastric pits there. The duodenal bulb has villi and crypts rather than gastric pits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands empty into deep gastric pits that occupy about half the thickness of the mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells in the antrum and duodenum secrete gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: pit depth and gland shape identify the gastric region on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology student examines glands that surround the esophageal orifice of the stomach. Which cell type predominates in these glands?","choices":{"A":"Mucous gland cells","B":"Zymogenic chief cells","C":"Parietal cells","D":"Enteroendocrine G cells","E":"Paneth cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glands around the esophageal orifice are cardiac glands, tubular and somewhat tortuous glands made up mostly of mucus-secreting cells with flattened basal nuclei and pale apical cytoplasm. Chief cells predominate in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck of fundic glands and appear only occasionally at the cardiac boundary. G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands. Paneth cells sit at the base of intestinal crypts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands are composed of mucus-secreting cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett metaplasia arises in the distal esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: mucous cardiac glands resemble the esophageal cardiac glands.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which cell type predominates in the deep base of the fundic glands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Surface mucous cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chief cells are concentrated in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck and upper part of the gland. Mucous neck cells are localized to the neck, among the parietal cells. Surface mucous cells line the surface and the gastric pits. Stem cells lie in the isthmus, between the pit and the gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin by acid (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: chief cells are basophilic on H&E.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which gastric epithelial cell secretes intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Surface mucous cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Parietal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, a 44-kDa glycoprotein that binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the distal ileum. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. Mucous neck cells secrete a soluble mucus that lubricates the gland lumen. Surface mucous cells secrete the insoluble alkaline mucus coat. ECL cells release histamine that stimulates parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (intrinsic factor is a 44-kDa glycoprotein).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells make gastric acid and intrinsic factor; autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor and an intact terminal ileum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"In H&E sections of fundic glands, chief cells show strongly basophilic basal cytoplasm. Which cellular feature produces this staining?","choices":{"A":"Abundant rough ER","B":"Intracellular canaliculi","C":"Apical mucin granules","D":"Abundant mitochondria","E":"Tubulovesicular membranes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chief cells have the morphology of protein-secreting cells: a basal nucleus, basal cytoplasm packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum whose ribosomal RNA stains basophilic, and apical zymogen granules. Abundant mitochondria, intracellular canaliculi, and a tubulovesicular membrane system are features of parietal cells and produce their eosinophilic staining. Apical mucin granules belong to surface mucous cells and appear pale on H&E. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (chief cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic (pink) and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: staining pattern reflects the dominant organelle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen as an inactive proenzyme. Which condition in the gastric lumen converts it to active pepsin?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin cleavage","B":"Enterokinase action","C":"Acidic pH below 5","D":"Bile salt binding","E":"Alkaline pH above 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen undergoes autocatalytic cleavage to pepsin on contact with acidic gastric juice at a pH below 5, with an optimum around pH 1.8 to 3.5; pepsin is an endopeptidase that starts protein digestion. Trypsin cleaves pancreatic zymogens in the duodenum, and enterokinase activates trypsinogen to trypsin. Bile salts emulsify fat rather than activate proteases. An alkaline pH inactivates pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pepsinogen and pepsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pepsinogen is converted to pepsin in the presence of H+ (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: acid suppression reduces gastric pepsin activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, what is the principal reason that even crystalline oral vitamin B12 is malabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric acid","B":"Loss of intrinsic factor","C":"Loss of bile secretion","D":"Loss of ileal receptors","E":"Loss of pancreatic proteases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parietal cells are the gastric source of intrinsic factor. When autoimmune destruction removes them, B12 does not form the B12 and intrinsic factor complex needed for receptor-mediated uptake in the distal ileum, which produces pernicious anemia. Crystalline B12 is not bound to food protein, so loss of gastric acid, which with pepsin frees dietary B12, does not explain its malabsorption. Bile secretion is unaffected by gastritis and does not carry B12 to the ileum. The ileal receptors are intact in this disease. Pancreatic proteases are not affected by gastritis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and to intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and raises the risk of pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: macrocytic anemia with neurologic signs suggests B12 deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis causes achlorhydria and markedly elevated serum gastrin. What causes the gastrin elevation?","choices":{"A":"Direct antibody stimulation of G cells","B":"Increased vagal drive to G cells","C":"Reduced renal gastrin clearance","D":"Gastrin release by ECL cells","E":"Loss of acid feedback on G cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Luminal acid normally provides negative feedback on antral G cells. With parietal cells destroyed, the antral lumen is no longer acidic, the feedback is lost, and G cells secrete gastrin continuously, which leads to G cell and ECL cell hyperplasia. The autoantibodies target parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, not G cells. Vagal drive is not the mechanism of the elevation. Renal clearance is not reduced in this disease. ECL cells release histamine, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis causes hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia, with risk of gastric cancer (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia with achlorhydria points away from gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic atrophic gastritis has lost gastric pepsin activity, yet protein digestion and absorption remain adequate. What explains this preserved digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Salivary proteases","C":"Gastric lipase activity","D":"Colonic bacterial enzymes","E":"Intestinal pepsin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases, including trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, work at neutral to alkaline pH in the duodenum and hydrolyze dietary protein into absorbable peptides and amino acids, so loss of gastric pepsin is compensated. Saliva contains amylase and lingual lipase rather than significant proteases. Gastric lipase digests fat. Colonic bacteria act after the main site of protein absorption. The intestine does not secrete pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, chief cell dysfunction and protein digestion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsinogen is activated by enterokinase and then activates the other pancreatic zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: protein malabsorption suggests pancreatic insufficiency rather than gastric loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"The unstirred layer beneath the gastric mucus gel stays near pH 7 while the lumen is near pH 1. Which component chiefly creates this alkaline microenvironment?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction sealing of cells","B":"Prostaglandin vasodilation","C":"Epithelial bicarbonate secretion","D":"Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel","E":"Rapid epithelial restitution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate into the fluid trapped beneath the mucus gel, creating an unstirred alkaline layer with a surface pH near 7 that neutralizes back-diffusing hydrogen ions and inactivates pepsin. Tight junctions prevent back-diffusion into the lamina propria but do not alkalinize the surface. Prostaglandins stimulate mucus and bicarbonate and maintain blood flow, but the alkaline layer itself is made by bicarbonate. Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel is a result of the alkaline layer, not its cause. Restitution repairs defects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial bicarbonate secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PGE2 increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion and decreases acid; NSAIDs remove this protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol restores prostaglandin protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Nonselective NSAIDs injure the gastric mucosa mainly by inhibiting cyclooxygenase. Which change follows?","choices":{"A":"Increased gastrin release","B":"Reduced PGE2 synthesis","C":"Urease ammonia production","D":"Blocked H2 receptors","E":"Direct vagal stimulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition suppresses PGE2 synthesis, which decreases bicarbonate and mucus secretion, reduces mucosal blood flow, and impairs restitution. Increased gastrin release follows loss of acid feedback, not COX inhibition. Urease ammonia production is a mechanism of H. pylori. Blocked H2 receptors would reduce acid and protect the mucosa. Direct vagal stimulation of acid occurs with brain injury in Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2, decreasing gastric mucosal protection; Curling ulcer follows burns and Cushing ulcer follows brain injury (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: co-prescribe misoprostol or a PPI for high-risk NSAID users.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Within minutes of a superficial gastric epithelial injury, the defect is sealed without new cell division. Which process accomplishes this?","choices":{"A":"Migration of neck cells","B":"Rapid stem cell mitosis","C":"Fibroblast scar formation","D":"Mucus plug secretion","E":"Platelet clot formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Restitution seals small epithelial defects as gland neck cells migrate along the basal lamina to restore continuity, without requiring immediate mitosis. Stem cell mitosis renews surface cells over days, not minutes. Fibroblast scarring follows deeper injury such as ulcers. Mucus slows pepsin and abrasion but does not close the epithelial gap. Platelet clotting stops bleeding but does not restore epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial restitution and continuous cell renewal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are limited to the mucosa and heal without scarring; ulcers extend deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: rapid restitution limits superficial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus changes abruptly at the esophagogastric junction to which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Stratified columnar","B":"Pseudostratified columnar","C":"Simple cuboidal","D":"Simple columnar","E":"Transitional"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction the stratified squamous lining changes abruptly to the simple columnar epithelium of the stomach, made up of surface mucous cells. Stratified columnar epithelium lines some large ducts. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airway. Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the thyroid follicles. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, lining epithelium); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophagogastric junction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus replaces squamous epithelium with intestinal columnar epithelium and goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the squamocolumnar junction is the endoscopic landmark for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Barrett metaplasia at the esophagogastric junction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis externa of the stomach differs from that of the esophagus by an additional layer. Which layer is this?","choices":{"A":"Outer circular layer","B":"Inner oblique layer","C":"Striated upper layer","D":"Middle longitudinal layer","E":"Submucosal muscle layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The stomach has three smooth muscle layers: an innermost oblique layer deep to the circular layer, an inner circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The esophagus has two layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal. The outer layer of both organs is longitudinal, not circular. The striated muscle of the upper esophagus is absent from the stomach. There is no middle longitudinal layer or submucosal muscle layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach adds an innermost oblique layer deep to the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 5; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid churning of chyme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — muscle type transitions along the esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike the esophagus, the stomach has no submucosal glands. In which layer do all gastric glands lie?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Adventitia","D":"Muscularis externa","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric glands are mucosal, confined to the lamina propria and opening into the bases of gastric pits; they include cardiac, fundic, and pyloric glands. The submucosa holds esophageal glands proper and duodenal Brunner glands, not gastric glands. The muscularis mucosae lies beneath the gland bases. The adventitia and muscularis externa are outer layers without glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (gastric mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands identify the esophagus and the duodenum (Brunner glands) on a slide (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: gland layer separates esophageal from gastric biopsies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal glands proper and cardiac glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy labeled gastric body. The section shows deep pits occupying half of the mucosa, coiled glands with wide lumina lined by mucous cells, and many gastrin-producing cells. Where was the biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Gastric body","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Pyloric antrum","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deep pits occupying about half of the mucosa, branched coiled glands with wide lumina, mucous lining cells, and abundant G cells define the pyloric region in the antrum. The fundus and body share fundic glands with shallow pits and many parietal and chief cells. The cardiac region has shallow pits over tubular mucous glands without concentrated G cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and submucosal Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyloric glands are lined mainly by mucus secreting cells (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: H. pylori gastritis affects the antrum first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — source and release triggers of gastrin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with vitiligo has fatigue and macrocytic anemia. Serum antibodies against the parietal cell proton pump are present. Which gastric region is the main target of this destruction?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and body","B":"Antrum and pylorus","C":"Cardia and esophagus","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Lesser curvature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Parietal cells are concentrated in the fundic glands of the fundus and body, so autoimmune gastritis targets these regions. The antrum and pylorus contain pyloric glands with few parietal cells and are spared; H. pylori gastritis begins there instead. The cardia has mucous cardiac glands, and the esophagus has squamous mucosa. The duodenal bulb is intestinal mucosa. The lesser curvature is an anatomic margin, not a gland region. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region, and section 3, parietal cell loss); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus; H. pylori affects the antrum first and spreads to the body (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune disease clusters, including vitiligo and thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis anatomic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man undergoes antrectomy for a bleeding ulcer. Afterward his serum gastrin falls well below his preoperative value. Removal of which cells explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Fundic ECL cells","B":"Pyloric G cells","C":"Cardiac mucous cells","D":"Fundic chief cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastrin-producing G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands of the antrum, so antrectomy removes the main source of gastrin. Fundic ECL cells release histamine and remain after antrectomy. Cardiac mucous cells secrete mucus. Fundic chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Duodenal S cells secrete secretin, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region, and section 2, enteroendocrine cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released by G cells in the antrum and duodenum in response to peptides, amino acids, and gastric distension (First Aid 2025 GI GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: antrectomy reduces gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — gastrin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy. In the neck of the fundic glands are large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm full of mitochondria and canaliculi. What do these cells secrete into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsinogen and lipase","B":"Soluble neck mucus","C":"Histamine","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Hydrochloric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large triangular eosinophilic cells with central nuclei, abundant mitochondria, and intracellular canaliculi in the gland neck are parietal cells. Their apical H+/K+-ATPase secretes hydrochloric acid, and they also secrete intrinsic factor. Pepsinogen and lipase come from basophilic chief cells in the gland base. Soluble neck mucus comes from mucous neck cells. Histamine is a paracrine product of ECL cells, and somatostatin of D cells, released toward the lamina propria rather than the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid, stimulated by histamine, ACh, and gastrin and inhibited by somatostatin (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: PPIs block the H+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — cellular mechanism of HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old healthy volunteer in a physiology study receives a gastrin infusion, which raises gastric acid output. Pretreatment with an H2 receptor blocker abolishes most of this rise. Gastrin acts mainly through which paracrine product?","choices":{"A":"D cell somatostatin","B":"Chief cell pepsinogen","C":"Mucous neck cell mucus","D":"ECL cell histamine","E":"Parietal cell intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin raises acid mainly by stimulating enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the fundic glands to release histamine, which acts on parietal cell H2 receptors; that is why H2 blockade abolishes most of the response. D cell somatostatin inhibits acid and gastrin release. Chief cell pepsinogen is a digestive proenzyme, not a paracrine signal. Mucous neck cell mucus lubricates the gland. Parietal cell intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells); First Aid 2025 GI (gastrin acts mainly through ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases acid secretion primarily through its effects on ECL cells, leading to histamine release, rather than through its direct effect on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — histamine and somatostatin control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy stained with PAS. Tall columnar cells lining the surface and pits have large apical cups that stain intensely. Their secretion contributes which component of gastric defense?","choices":{"A":"Soluble lubricating gland mucus","B":"Acid into the gland lumen","C":"Insoluble alkaline gel coat","D":"Pepsinogen for protein digestion","E":"Intrinsic factor for B12"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall columnar cells with PAS-positive apical mucous cups lining the surface and pits are surface mucous cells. They secrete an insoluble visible mucus rich in bicarbonate and potassium that forms a protective alkaline gel. Soluble lubricating mucus in the gland lumen comes from mucous neck cells, which lack prominent apical cups. Acid and intrinsic factor come from parietal cells. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins increase gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: the mucus layer limits pepsin contact with the epithelium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man had a total gastrectomy for cancer 6 years ago and takes no supplements. He now has numbness in both feet and decreased vibration sense, hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Absent gastric pepsin","B":"Ileal bacterial overgrowth","C":"Reduced folate absorption","D":"Pancreatic protease loss","E":"Absent intrinsic factor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Total gastrectomy removes all parietal cells, the gastric source of intrinsic factor. Without it, B12 is not absorbed in the distal ileum, and liver stores are depleted over several years, producing megaloblastic anemia and posterior and lateral column demyelination. Absent pepsin reduces release of B12 from food but is not the main cause, because intrinsic factor is also gone and supplements are needed even in crystalline form. Ileal bacterial overgrowth can follow gastric surgery but is not the main cause of this delayed, stores-depleted pattern. Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis without neurologic signs. Pancreatic proteases are preserved. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor; deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with subacute combined degeneration (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: give parenteral B12 after total gastrectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — pernicious anemia from loss of parietal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with antibodies to intrinsic factor has paresthesias, ataxia, and loss of position sense in both feet. Her vitamin B12 deficiency has gone untreated. Demyelination in which spinal cord region explains her neurologic signs?","choices":{"A":"Posterior and lateral columns","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","D":"Cerebellar vermis and flocculus","E":"Ventral nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe B12 deficiency causes demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord, producing sensory loss, ataxia, and weakness. Loss of position sense reflects the posterior columns, and the lateral columns carry the corticospinal tracts. Anterior horn neurons are lost in motor neuron diseases. Anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the classic target. The cerebellar vermis and flocculus are brain structures, not spinal cord regions. Ventral roots carry motor fibers and are not the primary site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, hematologic and neurologic impairment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration (Bootcamp Gastroenterology), with demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 3). COMLEX: neurologic damage may persist after treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — complications of autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing autoimmune atrophic gastritis has a serum gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: 0–100 pg/mL). Endoscopy shows small nodules in the gastric body, and biopsy shows proliferating small uniform cells with neuroendocrine granules. Which cell type is most likely proliferating?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"ECL cells","C":"D cells","D":"EC cells","E":"Ghrelin cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loss of acid removes negative feedback on antral G cells, and the resulting hypergastrinemia drives hyperplasia of the gastrin-responsive ECL cells in the body, which can form small nodules. G cells also become hyperplastic, but they lie in the antrum, not in body nodules. D cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin and acid, and are not driven by gastrin. EC cells release serotonin and are not the gastrin-responsive population named in the lecture. Ghrelin cells of the fundus modulate appetite and are not the cells that gastrin drives to proliferate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells, and section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis leads to hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia from achlorhydria is distinguished from gastrinoma by gastric pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — gastric neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with epigastric pain has a positive stool antigen test. Biopsy shows curved rods within the mucus over the surface foveolar cells. Which bacterial mechanism lets the organism survive at this acidic site?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate secretion","B":"Proton pump inhibition","C":"Gastrin receptor blockade","D":"Capsule blocking acid entry","E":"Urease generating ammonia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"H. pylori uses urease to convert urea into an ammonia cloud that buffers local acid, the basis of the urea breath test, and uses flagella to move through mucus. Bicarbonate is secreted by the host epithelium, not the bacterium. The organism does not inhibit the proton pump. The organism does not block gastrin receptors. A capsule is not its mechanism of acid survival. Its cytotoxins, such as CagA, then damage tight junctions and the mucus gel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is the most common cause of chronic gastritis, increases risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma, and affects the antrum first (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: urea breath test and stool antigen confirm infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with burns over 40% of his body surface area develops upper GI bleeding on day 3. Endoscopy shows multiple superficial gastric erosions. Failure of which barrier component is most responsible?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction proteins","B":"Surface cell turnover","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Mucus gel thickness","E":"Parietal cell number"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Reduced blood flow lowers oxygen and bicarbonate delivery and fails to wash away back-diffused hydrogen ions, leading to local acidosis, cell death, and erosions (Curling ulcer). Tight junctions are disrupted by H. pylori toxins and aspirin. Surface cell turnover and mucus thickness fall secondarily but are not the initiating failure. Parietal cell number is unchanged. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress); First Aid 2025 GI (Curling ulcer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curling ulcer follows burns from hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal stimulation and increased acid (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: give stress ulcer prophylaxis to critically ill patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute stress gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes high-dose aspirin for headaches and develops gastric erosions. Apart from inhibiting cyclooxygenase, aspirin injures the gastric surface by which direct action?","choices":{"A":"Damages hydrophobic membranes","B":"Stimulates gastrin release","C":"Blocks bicarbonate channels","D":"Activates pepsinogen in cells","E":"Raises vagal acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin directly damages the hydrophobic apical cell membranes, allowing acid to leak down its electrochemical gradient into the tissue, in addition to suppressing PGE2. It does not stimulate gastrin release. Its effect on bicarbonate comes through loss of prostaglandins, not channel blockade. Pepsinogen is activated in the lumen, not inside cells. Vagal acid output rises in Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2 and gastric mucosal protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol prevents NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID-associated ulcer prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a biopsy labeled distal esophagus. It shows simple columnar surface cells, gastric pits, and glands packed with numerous parietal and chief cells in the lamina propria, with no glands in the submucosa. Where was the tissue taken?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophagus","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Duodenal bulb","D":"Gastric body","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium, gastric pits, and mucosal glands rich in parietal and chief cells, without submucosal glands, define fundic mucosa of the gastric body. The distal esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and submucosal glands proper. The cardia has mucous glands with few parietal cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands in the submucosa. The pyloric antrum has deep pits over mucous glands with G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: a mislabeled specimen is identified by its epithelium and glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types along the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has leg edema and an albumin of 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Endoscopy shows giant folds in the body of the stomach resembling brain gyri, and biopsy shows foveolar hyperplasia with loss of parietal cells. Which normal structure is enlarged?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal villi","B":"Gastric rugae","C":"Plicae circulares","D":"Colonic haustra","E":"Esophageal papillae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric rugae are longitudinal folds of mucosa and submucosa that flatten when the stomach fills; in Ménétrier disease they become hypertrophied, with excess mucus production and protein loss. Intestinal villi are mucosal projections of the small intestine. Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestine. Colonic haustra are sacculations of the colon. Esophageal papillae are connective tissue projections into the esophageal epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization); First Aid 2025 GI (Ménétrier disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease is gastric mucosal hyperplasia with hypertrophied rugae, excess mucus, protein loss, and parietal cell atrophy with reduced acid (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: protein-losing gastropathy presents with edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hypertrophic gastropathy (Ménétrier disease)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist compares the luminal surfaces of the esophagus and stomach with a hand lens. Only the gastric surface is divided by shallow grooves into small, slightly bulging, cobblestone-like patches that contain mucosa alone. What are these patches called?","choices":{"A":"Gastric rugae","B":"Plicae circulares","C":"Mamillated areas","D":"Intestinal villi","E":"Haustral folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow grooves divide the gastric surface into slightly bulging patches called mamillated areas, which consist of mucosa only and slightly increase the surface area for secretion. The esophagus has a smooth surface with temporary longitudinal folds and no such areas. Gastric rugae are larger longitudinal folds that include submucosa and flatten as the stomach fills. Plicae circulares are permanent folds of the small intestine. Intestinal villi are finger-like mucosal projections of the small bowel. Haustral folds belong to the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization, p20); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mamillated areas consist only of mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach mucosa shows rugae, mamillated areas, and gastric pits, while the esophagus lacks gastric pits (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: rugae flatten when the stomach is distended (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — gastric pits and regional glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has an endoscopic biopsy taken just below the gastroesophageal junction. It shows shallow gastric pits over tubular, somewhat tortuous mucous glands with only occasional parietal cells. Which gastric region was sampled?","choices":{"A":"Fundic region","B":"Pyloric region","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow pits over tubular, tortuous glands composed mostly of mucous cells, with occasional parietal cells at the border, define the cardiac region around the esophageal orifice. The fundic region has shallow pits over long glands full of parietal and chief cells. The pyloric region has deep pits that occupy about half of the mucosa. The distal esophagus is lined by stratified squamous epithelium without gastric pits. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cardia is the junction zone where Barrett metaplasia is assessed (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: biopsy site matters when reporting intestinal metaplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal cardiac glands","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man takes a drug that irreversibly blocks the apical H+/K+-ATPase of large cells in the neck of the fundic glands. Which feature identifies these cells on H&E staining?","choices":{"A":"Intensely basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Clear apical mucous granule cups","C":"Flattened nuclei at the cell base","D":"Apical zymogen granule clusters","E":"Intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The drug targets parietal cells, large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria and canaliculi. Basophilic basal cytoplasm and apical zymogen granules identify chief cells, whose apical cytoplasm is also eosinophilic but less intensely. Clear apical mucous cups identify surface mucous cells. Flattened basal nuclei are typical of mucous gland cells in cardiac glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: PPIs heal 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — proton pump inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia. Her B12 absorption returns to normal when oral B12 is given together with intrinsic factor. Where is the B12 and intrinsic factor complex normally absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Distal ileum","B":"Duodenal bulb","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells binds B12, and the complex is taken up by receptor mediated endocytosis in the distal ileum, so an intact ileum explains why added intrinsic factor corrects absorption. The duodenal bulb and proximal jejunum absorb iron, folate, and most nutrients but not the B12 complex. The gastric antrum does not absorb B12. The ascending colon absorbs water and electrolytes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, B12 and intrinsic factor uptake in the distal ileum, lines 596 and 632); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor and causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with parenteral B12.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old woman develops hematemesis after a night of binge drinking. Endoscopy shows superficial gastric erosions. How does ethanol injure the gastric surface?","choices":{"A":"COX enzyme inhibition","B":"Urease ammonia production","C":"Vagal acid stimulation","D":"Direct cell toxicity","E":"Gastrin hypersecretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alcohol injures the gastric surface by direct chemical cytotoxicity, leading to local mucosal acidosis and cell death. COX inhibition is the mechanism of NSAIDs. Urease ammonia production is how H. pylori buffers acid to survive, not how ethanol injures the mucosa. Vagal acid stimulation causes Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Gastrin hypersecretion occurs with a gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute gastritis risk factors include alcohol use disorder and daily NSAID use (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: forceful retching can also cause Mallory-Weiss tears.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has a resection specimen from the upper abdomen. The organ has simple columnar epithelium, three layers of smooth muscle in the muscularis externa, and a serosal covering. Which organ is this?","choices":{"A":"Esophagus","B":"Stomach","C":"Duodenum","D":"Jejunum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with an extra innermost oblique layer, giving three muscle layers, and a serosal coat is characteristic of the stomach. The esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium, two muscle layers, and mostly adventitia. The duodenum and jejunum have villi and two muscle layers. The colon has crypts and teniae coli in two muscle layers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid gastric churning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has dyspepsia, and gastric biopsy shows curved rods on the foveolar cells with chronic active inflammation. The strain carries the CagA gene. How does this toxin weaken the mucosal barrier?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction disruption","B":"COX enzyme inhibition","C":"Reduced mucosal blood flow","D":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","E":"Vagal overstimulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"H. pylori cytotoxins such as CagA destroy tight junctions, disrupt the mucus gel, and trigger chronic active inflammation, letting acid and pepsin reach the epithelium. COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism. Reduced mucosal blood flow is the mechanism of shock and burns. Parietal cell autoimmunity causes autoimmune gastritis. Vagal overstimulation causes Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori increases the risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with triple therapy and confirm eradication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl03_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis and vitiligo has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, and numbness in both feet. Biopsy of the gastric body shows thin mucosa with few glands, while the antrum looks normal. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Serum B12"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↓","↓"],"C":["↔","↔","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells in the body and fundus, so the pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) is correct. Acid falls, gastrin from the spared antrum rises, and serum B12 falls, which fits the glossitis and neuropathy. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), in which tumor gastrin drives intact parietal cells to secrete excess acid; intrinsic factor is still made, so B12 is typically normal, and although very low duodenal pH can inactivate the pancreatic proteases that cleave R-binder and free B12 to bind intrinsic factor (inferred: Costanzo describes low duodenal pH inactivating pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and pancreatic proteases degrading R proteins), the high acid level excludes this row whatever the B12 value. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is folate deficiency, which causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia but leaves gastric secretion and B12 unchanged and does not explain the numbness in her feet. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows total gastrectomy, which removes the antral G cells, so gastrin falls, and removes the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are lost and B12 is not absorbed; the low gastrin separates this row from the key. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows resection of the distal ileum, where the B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed, with normal gastric function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (pernicious anemia); Guyton supplemental (folic acid deficiency causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; gastrectomy removes the source of intrinsic factor, so B12 must be injected); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, total or partial gastrectomy causes B12 deficiency); the low gastrin in row D is inferred from removal of the antrum, and the B12 value in row A is inferred from preserved intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and causes hypochlorhydria, hypergastrinemia, and pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: a high gastrin level with low acid points to atrophic gastritis or acid suppression rather than gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}],"4":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa, contraction of the inner circular layer has which effect on a gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Narrows the lumen","B":"Shortens the segment","C":"Folds the mucosa","D":"Widens the lumen","E":"Lengthens the segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Contraction of the inner circular layer decreases the luminal diameter. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the segment. Folding of the mucosa is produced by the muscularis mucosae. Widening of the lumen follows relaxation of circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and lengthening follows relaxation of longitudinal muscle; neither is produced by circular contraction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, inner circular muscle layer); Costanzo Ch. 8 (circular muscle decreases diameter, longitudinal muscle decreases length).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two muscle layers controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: circular and longitudinal layers are reciprocally innervated during peristalsis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the gut wall alters the folding and surface area of the inner lining to aid local absorption and secretion?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Outer longitudinal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Lamina propria","E":"Serosal layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer at the base of the mucosa whose local contractions change mucosal shape and surface area. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa move the whole segment. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with vessels and immune cells. The serosa is not the layer described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"During peristalsis, when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, what does the longitudinal muscle of that segment do?","choices":{"A":"Contracts","B":"Relaxes","C":"Is unchanged","D":"Fatigues","E":"Depolarizes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Circular and longitudinal muscle are reciprocally innervated, so when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, its longitudinal muscle relaxes, and the segment narrows and lengthens behind the bolus. Simultaneous contraction is what reciprocal innervation prevents. The longitudinal layer changes its tone rather than staying unchanged. Fatigue is not part of the reflex. Depolarization would lead to contraction, not relaxation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (reciprocal innervation and peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts; ahead of it, circular muscle relaxes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the segment ahead of the bolus relaxes so the bolus can advance (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the gastrointestinal tract synapse in which location?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Submucosal plexus","C":"Dorsal root ganglia","D":"Inferior vagal ganglion","E":"Prevertebral ganglia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short and synapse outside the gut in four prevertebral ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses are where parasympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse and where sympathetic postganglionic fibers end. Dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic synapse sites. The inferior vagal ganglion is not a sympathetic synapse site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic splanchnic supply is T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T12 for the midgut, and L1 to L2 for the hindgut (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture also lists paravertebral chain ganglia in the sympathetic pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits the parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that supply the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Short with prevertebral ganglia","B":"Short with intramural ganglia","C":"Long with chain ganglia","D":"Long with intramural ganglia","E":"Short with chain ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long, running from the brainstem or sacral cord to the gut, and synapse in ganglia within the wall, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses, so the postganglionic fibers are short. Short preganglionic fibers synapsing in prevertebral ganglia describe the sympathetic supply. Short fibers ending in the wall describe no autonomic division. Chain ganglia are sympathetic paravertebral ganglia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic ganglia lie in or near the target organ; sympathetic ganglia lie outside it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal preganglionic fibers synapse in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Activation of sympathetic postganglionic fibers to the gut produces which effect?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of sphincters","B":"Relaxation of sphincters","C":"Increased gland secretion","D":"Increased wall motility","E":"Mucosal vasodilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sympathetic activity generally inhibits gut function: it relaxes the smooth muscle of the walls, contracts GI sphincters, decreases motility, inhibits secretion, and constricts GI vessels. Relaxation of sphincters, increased gland secretion, and increased wall motility are parasympathetic effects. Mucosal vasodilation accompanies parasympathetic stimulation during digestion, whereas sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic stimulation inhibits activity in the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norepinephrine inhibits gut motility, mostly through inhibition of enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: intense sympathetic activity can stop movement of food through the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"The pelvic nerve supplies parasympathetic fibers to which part of the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and proximal duodenum","B":"Jejunum and terminal ileum","C":"Cecum and ascending colon","D":"Upper third of esophagus","E":"Descending and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pelvic nerve, from sacral segments S2 to S4, supplies the lower GI tract: the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal. The stomach, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon receive vagal parasympathetic fibers. The upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle supplied by vagal fibers from the nucleus ambiguus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation; Costanzo lists the transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, and the lecture adds the rectum and anal canal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut parasympathetic supply is from S2 to S4 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers coordinate defecation reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, besides coordinating defecation reflexes, which colonic function does parasympathetic outflow through the pelvic nerve stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Mucus secretion","B":"Water absorption","C":"Bile acid uptake","D":"Motility inhibition","E":"Sphincter contraction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pelvic nerve fibers from S2 to S4 are heavily concentrated in the sigmoid, rectal, and anal regions, where they coordinate defecation reflexes and stimulate colonic mucus secretion. Water absorption by the colonic epithelium is not the function the lecture assigns to pelvic outflow. Bile acid uptake occurs mainly in the terminal ileum, which is in vagal territory. Parasympathetic activity increases rather than inhibits colonic motility. Sphincter contraction is a sympathetic effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve distribution and functions, lines 843–850; section 2, sympathetic contraction of sphincters); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that parasympathetic activity increases peristalsis and secretions while lowering sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach physiology). COMLEX: sacral cord lesions impair defecation reflexes and colonic mucus secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most distal part of the gastrointestinal tract that receives vagal parasympathetic fibers?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Proximal transverse colon","C":"Proximal descending colon","D":"Proximal sigmoid colon","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vagus supplies the esophagus, stomach, pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid region are supplied by the pelvic nerve. This boundary matches the midgut and hindgut junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus nerve distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut parasympathetic supply is the vagus; hindgut supply is S2 to S4 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the distal third of the transverse colon is hindgut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"About what proportion of the fibers in the vagus nerve are afferent?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that about 75 percent of vagal fibers are afferent and 25 percent efferent; the lecture gives 75 to 80 percent afferent. The 25 percent figure is the efferent fraction. About 50 percent does not match the vagal split. About 10 and 90 percent match no source. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagus is 75% afferent and 25% efferent); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, structural composition).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes carry both afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents relay stretch and chemical signals from the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"In a vagovagal reflex, the vagal afferent fibers from the gut terminate in which brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","B":"Nucleus ambiguus of medulla","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus","E":"Hypoglossal motor nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal afferents from the gut end in the nucleus tractus solitarius of the medulla. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is the origin of the efferent limb. The nucleus ambiguus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and hypoglossal nucleus are not the termination site of vagal afferents in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent and efferent pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in vagovagal reflexes both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: receptive relaxation and distension-driven acid secretion are vagovagal reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Distension of the orad stomach triggers a vagovagal reflex that relaxes the gastric wall. Which transmitters do the postganglionic neurons release?","choices":{"A":"ACh and substance P","B":"VIP and nitric oxide","C":"Gastrin and CCK","D":"Secretin and somatostatin","E":"GRP and histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which peptidergic postganglionic neurons release VIP and nitric oxide, relaxing the orad stomach so it can hold up to 1.5 L without a rise in pressure. ACh and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Gastrin and CCK are hormones, not reflex transmitters. Secretin and somatostatin are a hormone and a paracrine inhibitor. GRP stimulates G cells, and histamine is a paracrine stimulant of parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (receptive relaxation; the neurotransmitter is VIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"In the peristaltic reflex, intrinsic primary afferent neurons in the gut wall receive chemical activation from which signal?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers","B":"ACh from vagal efferent fibers","C":"Gastrin from antral G endocrine cells","D":"Histamine from mucosal mast cells","E":"Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bolus stimulates enterochromaffin cells in the mucosa to release serotonin, which binds intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) and starts the peristaltic reflex in that segment. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers suppresses enteric activity. ACh from vagal efferent fibers is extrinsic modulation, not the local trigger. Gastrin from antral G cells is a hormone acting on parietal cells. Histamine from mast cells is not the trigger described in the lecture. IPANs also respond directly to stretch. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sensorimotor architecture); Costanzo Ch. 8 (5-HT binds IPANs to initiate the peristaltic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system contains more than 100 million neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: local reflexes work without central processing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"During the peristaltic reflex, excitatory enteric neurons contract the circular muscle behind the bolus. Which transmitters do they release?","choices":{"A":"VIP and nitric oxide","B":"Norepinephrine and dopamine","C":"Secretin and CCK","D":"ACh and substance P","E":"Gastrin and histamine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excitatory enteric neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract muscle behind the bolus. VIP and nitric oxide are released by inhibitory neurons that relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax sphincters. Norepinephrine is released by extrinsic sympathetic fibers and inhibits motility. Secretin and CCK are hormones. Gastrin and histamine regulate acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity, and norepinephrine usually inhibits it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs slow gut motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers suppress gut motility and secretion. Where do most of these fibers end?","choices":{"A":"On smooth muscle cells","B":"On mucosal epithelium","C":"On enteric neurons","D":"On vagal ganglia","E":"On dorsal root ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on neurons of the enteric nervous system, where norepinephrine inhibits transmitter release; only a small share acts directly on smooth muscle and vessels. Smooth muscle cells receive a minor direct input. Mucosal epithelium is controlled mainly through enteric neurons. Vagal ganglia and dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation inhibits gut activity mainly through the enteric nervous system (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: sympathetic activity also contracts sphincters and constricts gut vessels (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a full-thickness colon biopsy for chronic constipation. Ganglion cells are seen between the dense connective tissue of the submucosa and the inner circular muscle layer. Which function does this network mainly control?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa contraction","B":"Relaxation of the GI sphincters","C":"Local secretion and blood flow","D":"Initiation of the swallow reflex","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the submucosal connective tissue and the inner circular layer form the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls local fluid and electrolyte secretion, absorption, local blood flow, and movement of the muscularis mucosae. Contraction of the muscularis externa and relaxation of sphincters are controlled by the myenteric plexus. The swallow reflex is initiated by the medullary swallowing center. Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex of the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosal plexus location, and section 5, submucosal plexus control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus integrates epithelial sensory signals to control fluid secretion and local vasodilation (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus also controls the muscularis mucosae (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has an endoscopic submucosal injection before removal of a colon polyp. The needle tip lies in dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels, just deep to the muscularis mucosae. Which neural network lies in this layer?","choices":{"A":"Auerbach plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Vagal trunk plexus","D":"Meissner plexus","E":"Pelvic plexus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa, which contains the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The Auerbach (myenteric) plexus lies deeper, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. The celiac and pelvic plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. Vagal trunks lie on the esophagus and stomach, outside the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosa and submucosal plexus); First Aid 2025 GI (digestive tract anatomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosa contains the Meissner plexus and vessels; the muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosa carries the larger vessels of the gut wall (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman in a pharmacology study receives an agent that blocks synaptic transmission in the ganglia between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Which function is reduced?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic wave velocity","B":"Mucosal fluid secretion","C":"Muscularis mucosae folding","D":"Local mucosal vasodilation","E":"Epithelial absorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglia between the two muscle layers form the myenteric plexus, which sets smooth muscle tone, the rate and intensity of rhythmic contractions, and the conduction velocity of peristaltic waves. Mucosal fluid secretion, muscularis mucosae folding, and local vasodilation are controlled by the submucosal plexus. Epithelial absorption depends on enterocytes and submucosal control, not on the myenteric plexus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, myenteric plexus location, and section 5, motility control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Auerbach plexus controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: loss of myenteric neurons causes achalasia in the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — enteric control of esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has episodes of severe hypertension, headaches, and sweating, and imaging shows an adrenal mass. She also reports constipation. Which gastrointestinal effect of her excess catecholamine most likely contributes to the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Faster transit with relaxed sphincters","B":"Slow transit and tight sphincters","C":"Increased glandular fluid secretion","D":"Mucosal vasodilation and hyperemia","E":"Destruction of myenteric neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norepinephrine, the sympathetic postganglionic transmitter, relaxes the smooth muscle of the gut wall, contracts sphincters, decreases motility and transit, and inhibits secretion, which together most likely favor constipation; Robbins does not list constipation among pheochromocytoma features, so the clinical outcome is inferred from the mechanism. Faster transit with relaxed sphincters and increased glandular secretion are parasympathetic effects. Sympathetic activity constricts, rather than dilates, gut vessels. Norepinephrine inhibits enteric neurons but does not destroy them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine usually inhibits GI activity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity inhibits gut motility and secretion and contracts sphincters (Costanzo Ch. 8; Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: norepinephrine acts mainly through enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman takes an overdose of a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has a dry mouth, flushed skin, and absent bowel sounds. Which normal parasympathetic action has been blocked?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscle","B":"Vasoconstriction of gut vessels","C":"Inhibition of glandular secretion","D":"Contraction of gut smooth muscle","E":"Release of norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic cholinergic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors to increase smooth muscle contraction and motility, relax sphincters, and increase glandular secretion. Blocking muscarinic receptors removes these effects, causing ileus and dry mouth. Relaxation of gut smooth muscle, vasoconstriction, and inhibition of secretion are sympathetic effects. Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic, not parasympathetic, fibers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, neurotransmitters and effects on GI function).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic activity relaxes GI sphincters (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — parasympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with pancreatic cancer has severe abdominal pain and receives a celiac plexus block. The injected ganglion lies outside the gut wall in front of the aorta. Which neurons have cell bodies in this ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons","B":"Vagal preganglionic neurons","C":"Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","D":"Enteric interneurons of the wall","E":"Dorsal root sensory neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral sympathetic ganglion where short preganglionic fibers synapse on postganglionic neurons whose long adrenergic fibers run to the gut. Parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies lie in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses within the gut wall. Vagal preganglionic cell bodies lie in the dorsal motor nucleus of the brainstem, and vagal fibers pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing; Bootcamp labels the celiac ganglia parasympathetic, but the lecture lists them as sympathetic prevertebral ganglia. Enteric interneurons lie in the gut wall. Dorsal root sensory neurons do not lie in the celiac ganglion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location and fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut sympathetic supply runs through the T5 to T9 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture lists the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric ganglia as prevertebral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"Which of the following digestive organs receives its parasympathetic supply from the cranial division rather than from the S2 to S4 sacral division?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Rectum","D":"Anal canal","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vagus nerve, the cranial division, supplies parasympathetic fibers to the esophagus, stomach, exocrine pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The pelvic nerve from S2 to S4 supplies the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, the rectum, and the anal canal, so the sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal, and descending colon all depend on the sacral outflow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, p24); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal supply ends at the proximal transverse colon, and S2 to S4 supply begins at the distal transverse colon (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: pelvic nerve outflow is concentrated in the sigmoid, rectum, and anus to coordinate defecation (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — vagal acetylcholine drives the cephalic phase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman undergoes truncal vagotomy, with both vagal trunks divided at the esophageal hiatus, during surgery for recurrent bleeding from a duodenal ulcer. Her sacral spinal cord and pelvic nerves are intact. Which segment of the gut loses its parasympathetic supply?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Ascending colon","C":"Descending colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing both vagal trunks removes parasympathetic supply from the whole vagal territory, which runs from the esophagus and stomach through the small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and anal canal receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic nerve, which arises from S2 to S4 and is intact in this patient. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, lines 835–850); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the vagus as the parasympathetic nerve of the foregut and of the midgut, which extends to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: after truncal vagotomy, the left colon still receives sacral parasympathetic outflow (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — anterior and posterior vagal trunks","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with a sacral myelomeningocele has absent function of the S2 to S4 cord segments. Which gastrointestinal function is most affected?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid secretion","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion","D":"Small bowel peristalsis","E":"Rectal defecation reflexes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pelvic nerve innervation from S2 to S4 is concentrated in the sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal, where it coordinates defecation reflexes and stimulates colonic mucus secretion. Gastric acid secretion, gallbladder contraction, and pancreatic enzyme secretion receive vagal parasympathetic input. Small bowel peristalsis is driven by the myenteric plexus with vagal modulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pelvic nerve arises from the second, third, and fourth sacral cord segments (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: neurogenic bowel follows sacral cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — hindgut derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old man has early satiety and epigastric fullness after small meals since a truncal vagotomy. Loss of which reflex best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Peristaltic reflex","E":"Defecation reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which distension of the orad stomach relaxes the gastric wall so it can hold a meal without a rise in pressure; vagotomy eliminates it, causing early fullness. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying in response to duodenal contents, and its loss would not cause early satiety. The peristaltic reflex is a local enteric reflex that survives vagotomy. The defecation reflex is sacral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex mediated by VIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired gastric accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — receptive relaxation of the orad stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman in a physiology study has a balloon inflated in her stomach, and her serum gastrin rises. Postganglionic vagal neurons release which transmitter onto the antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"VIP","B":"ACh","C":"CCK","D":"NO","E":"GRP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric distension initiates vagovagal reflexes in which postganglionic neurons release ACh onto parietal cells and gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto antral G cells, increasing acid secretion. VIP and nitric oxide relax the orad stomach in receptive relaxation. ACh is the transmitter acting on parietal cells rather than on G cells. CCK is a hormone from duodenal I cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation releases GRP to increase gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: atropine does not block vagal stimulation of gastrin because GRP is not cholinergic (First Aid 2025 GI acid secretion diagram: atropine acts on the ACh limb).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — neural regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman notices an urge to defecate within minutes after each large meal. Which reflex is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Rectosphincteric reflex","E":"Vomiting reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric distension triggers the gastrocolic reflex, a long arc reflex that increases colonic motility and starts mass movements. The lecture describes it as a long arc vagovagal reflex, which is the exam convention; Costanzo instead places the afferent limb in parasympathetic nerves and the efferent limb in CCK and gastrin. Receptive relaxation relaxes the stomach itself. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter when the rectum fills, after mass movements arrive. The vomiting reflex is coordinated in the medulla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastrocolic reflex); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrocolic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility and mass movements after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: mass movements carry colonic contents toward the rectum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has a segment of jejunum studied after all of its extrinsic nerves are cut. Stroking the mucosa still triggers a local peristaltic reflex. Where are the cell bodies of the sensory neurons that detect this stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Nodose ganglion","C":"Prevertebral ganglia","D":"Within the gut submucosa","E":"Nucleus tractus solitarius"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) are embedded in the submucosa. They detect stretch or chemical stimuli, often after enterochromaffin cells release serotonin, and signal enteric interneurons that coordinate motor, secretory, and endocrine responses without central processing, so the reflex survives extrinsic denervation. Dorsal root ganglia and the nodose ganglion hold the cell bodies of extrinsic spinal and vagal afferents, which were cut. Prevertebral ganglia hold sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The nucleus tractus solitarius is the medullary site where vagal afferents end. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, IPANs embedded in the submucosa, p25; section 4, vagal afferents end in the nucleus tractus solitarius, p24).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system has over 100 million intrinsic neurons and can run local reflexes on its own (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: extrinsic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers modulate, but are not needed for, intrinsic reflexes (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents in the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has paralytic ileus after a long abdominal operation with intense sympathetic activation. Norepinephrine suppresses his motility mainly through which effect on the enteric nervous system?","choices":{"A":"Increased vagal outflow","B":"Loss of enteric neurons","C":"Blocked nicotinic receptors","D":"Increased VIP and NO release","E":"Reduced enteric ACh release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons, and norepinephrine binds presynaptic receptors that inhibit release of excitatory transmitters such as ACh, suppressing motility. Increased vagal outflow would increase motility. Enteric neurons are inhibited, not lost. Norepinephrine does not block nicotinic receptors. Increased VIP and NO release would relax muscle by a different pathway and is not the sympathetic mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (inhibitory effect of norepinephrine on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strong sympathetic stimulation can inhibit gut movement enough to block passage of food (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes also use prevertebral sympathetic ganglia (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — sympathetic limb of the enterogastric reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman is studied after her extrinsic gut nerves are blocked pharmacologically. Luminal peptides still increase gastrin release from her antral G cells. Which neural element can still modulate the G cells?","choices":{"A":"Vagal afferent fibers","B":"Prevertebral ganglia","C":"Enteric interneurons","D":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers","E":"Spinal motor neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enteric interneurons synapse with and modulate enteroendocrine cells, regulating the release of local hormones such as gastrin, CCK, secretin, and somatostatin in response to luminal conditions, even without extrinsic input. Vagal afferents, prevertebral sympathetic ganglia, and pelvic splanchnic fibers are extrinsic and are blocked. Spinal motor neurons supply skeletal muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, endocrine modulation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells release gastrin in response to small peptides and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: the enteric nervous system functions independently of extrinsic nerves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — gastrin release triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has a segment of ileum resected and examined. Which layer lies immediately outside its outer longitudinal muscle?","choices":{"A":"Mucosa","B":"Serosa","C":"Adventitia","D":"Submucosa","E":"Muscularis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intraperitoneal segments such as the ileum are covered by a serosa, a serous membrane of simple squamous mesothelium outside the outer longitudinal muscle. The mucosa and submucosa lie inside the muscularis externa. Adventitia is the connective tissue coat of fixed, retroperitoneal segments. The muscularis is the muscle layer itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, serosa or adventitia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gut wall layers from lumen outward are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: retroperitoneal segments have an adventitia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has midgut visceral pain carried with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12. In which ganglion do the preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the midgut synapse?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","B":"Superior cervical ganglion","C":"Hypogastric plexus ganglion","D":"Superior mesenteric ganglion","E":"Myenteric plexus ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12, which synapse in the superior mesenteric ganglion wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery; its postganglionic axons reach the territory of that artery. The inferior mesenteric and hypogastric ganglia serve the hindgut and pelvis. The superior cervical ganglion serves the head and neck. Myenteric ganglia are intrinsic. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (midgut: SMA, T10 to T12); Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (superior mesenteric ganglion, lines 3976–3981).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut has vagal parasympathetic and T10 to T12 splanchnic sympathetic supply (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: hindgut sympathetic supply is L1 to L2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl eats a small protein meal that barely distends her stomach, yet a vagovagal reflex raises her acid secretion. Which receptors most likely start this reflex?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal chemoreceptors","B":"Wall stretch receptors","C":"Visceral pain nociceptors","D":"Mucosal thermoreceptors","E":"Carotid baroreceptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vagovagal afferents start from mechanoreceptors that detect stretch or distension and from chemoreceptors that detect pH, osmolarity, nutrients, or mucosal irritation; with little distension, digestion products act on mucosal chemoreceptors. Stretch receptors need distension. Pain nociceptors are not the afferents of this reflex. Thermoreceptors and carotid baroreceptors are not afferents of this reflex either. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent pathway and gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes stimulate parietal cells through ACh and G cells through GRP (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: luminal digestion products trigger the gastric phase of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 83-year-old man has a vagovagal reflex studied after a meal. Where are the cell bodies of the vagal afferent neurons that carry the sensory limb?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion","B":"Myenteric plexus","C":"Geniculate ganglion","D":"Ventral horn","E":"Nodose ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Afferent axons traveling with the vagus have their cell bodies in the nodose (inferior vagal) ganglion, and their central processes end in the solitary nucleus of the medulla. The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The geniculate ganglion belongs to the facial nerve. The ventral horn holds motor neurons. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (vagal afferent cell bodies in the nodose ganglion, lines 2891–2893 and 5126–5127).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a vagovagal reflex both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents also sense plasma glucose and portal osmotic pressure (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — medullary swallowing center","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old woman joins a motility study of the enteric neurons that relax muscle ahead of a bolus. Which transmitter is released by these inhibitory motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"ACh","B":"GRP","C":"ATP","D":"CCK","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inhibitory enteric motor neurons use ATP among other cotransmitters to relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax GI sphincters. ACh is excitatory and contracts muscle behind the bolus. GRP stimulates G cells. CCK and gastrin are hormones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric plexus regulates muscle tone and peristaltic wave velocity (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: excitatory enteric neurons contract muscle behind a bolus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — LES relaxation ahead of the bolus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has visceral abdominal pain carried by afferent fibers that run with the splanchnic sympathetic nerves. Where are the cell bodies of these afferent neurons?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Inferior vagal ganglion","C":"Celiac ganglion","D":"Myenteric plexus","E":"Dorsal horn of cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Visceral afferent fibers associated with the splanchnic nerves have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia of spinal nerves, and their central processes end in the dorsal horn. The inferior vagal (nodose) ganglion holds vagal afferent cell bodies. The celiac ganglion holds sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The dorsal horn receives the central processes rather than the cell bodies. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (visceral afferents with splanchnic nerves, lines 2888–2890 and 5137–5138).","boardPrep":"Step 1: splanchnic visceral afferents end in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6). COMLEX: about half of sympathetic nerve fibers are afferent (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true}],"5":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, how does a GI paracrine reach its target cell?","choices":{"A":"Portal then systemic circulation","B":"Release into a synaptic cleft","C":"Local interstitial diffusion","D":"Secretion into the gut lumen","E":"Retrograde axonal transport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paracrines are secreted by endocrine cells of the GI mucosa and act locally, diffusing short distances through interstitial fluid to adjacent target cells. Travel through the portal and then systemic circulation describes hormones. Release into a synaptic cleft after an action potential describes neurocrines. Secretion into the lumen describes exocrine products such as enzymes. Retrograde axonal transport carries signals within neurons, not between cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (classification of GI peptides).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin and histamine are the classic GI paracrines (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: H2 blockers interrupt a paracrine signal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which GI peptide acts as a neurocrine released by enteric neurons?","choices":{"A":"GIP","B":"VIP","C":"CCK","D":"PYY","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is synthesized in enteric neurons and released at synapses, so it is a neurocrine. GIP, CCK, and gastrin are official GI hormones secreted by K, I, and G cells into the blood. Peptide YY is a candidate hormone from L cells of the ileum and colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: VIP relaxes GI smooth muscle and sphincters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"Which paracrine is released by enterochromaffin-like cells in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Somatostatin","C":"Gastrin","D":"Secretin","E":"Ghrelin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ECL cells in the oxyntic region release histamine, which diffuses to neighboring parietal cells and binds H2 receptors. Somatostatin is a paracrine, but it comes from D cells. Gastrin is a hormone from antral G cells. Secretin is a hormone from duodenal S cells. Ghrelin is a hormone from gastric oxyntic cells that acts on the hypothalamus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines, and section 6, ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells through a Gs cascade (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 receptor antagonists reduce acid by blocking this paracrine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, S cells release secretin in response to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Amino acids in the stomach","B":"Glucose in the jejunum","C":"Distension of the antrum","D":"Vagal release of GRP","E":"Acid in the duodenum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when acidic chyme below pH 4.5 enters the duodenum, and fatty acids also stimulate it. Amino acids in the stomach, antral distension, and vagal GRP release are stimuli for gastrin. Glucose in the small intestine stimulates GIP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- secretion and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin also inhibits gastrin release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"Fatty acids entering the duodenum release a hormone from I cells that contracts the gallbladder. Which hormone is this?","choices":{"A":"CCK","B":"PYY","C":"VIP","D":"GIP","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I cells of the duodenum and jejunum in response to monoglycerides, fatty acids, small peptides, and amino acids; it contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion. GIP comes from K cells and stimulates insulin release. PYY comes from L cells and decreases appetite. VIP is a neurocrine. Gastrin comes from G cells and stimulates acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals provoke biliary colic through CCK.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, one GI hormone is secreted in response to all three nutrient types: glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"I cells","B":"S cells","C":"K cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GIP is secreted by K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa and, unlike the other GI hormones, is released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. I cells secrete CCK in response to fats and protein products. S cells secrete secretin in response to acid and fatty acids. G cells secrete gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids. L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP is an incretin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome can cause steatorrhea. Which mechanism explains it?","choices":{"A":"Loss of bile acid secretion","B":"Acid inactivation of lipase","C":"Gastrin block of CCK release","D":"Villous atrophy from gluten","E":"Bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, excess acid acidifies the intestinal lumen and inactivates pancreatic lipase, so dietary fat is not digested and appears in the stool. Bile acid secretion is not reduced by gastrin. Gastrin does not block CCK release. Villous atrophy from gluten is celiac disease. Bacterial overgrowth causes malabsorption by a different mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrinoma presents with refractory ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastrinoma is associated with MEN1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, chronically high gastrin levels change the gastric oxyntic mucosa in which way?","choices":{"A":"Atrophy and parietal loss","B":"Intestinal type metaplasia","C":"Fibrosis and scarring of glands","D":"Necrosis and mucosal sloughing","E":"Hypertrophy and hyperplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastrin has a trophic effect, so chronically elevated gastrin causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa with marked parietal cell hyperplasia. Atrophy with parietal cell loss occurs after antrectomy or in autoimmune gastritis. Intestinal metaplasia follows chronic gastritis. Fibrosis and necrosis are not effects of gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases growth of the gastric mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: thickened gastric folds with refractory ulcers suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptic ulcer location most strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal bulb","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal jejunum","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome causes severe recurrent ulcers in the duodenum and stomach and frequently in unusual distal sites such as the jejunum, because excess unbuffered acid reaches the small intestine. Duodenal bulb ulcers are common in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome but also in ordinary H. pylori disease, so they are less specific. Lesser curvature and antral ulcers are typical gastric ulcers. The cardia is an uncommon ulcer site with no link to gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, ulcer location).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are most common in gastrinoma, but jejunal ulcers are the most specific clue (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: ulcers that do not respond to therapy suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Oral glucose releases more insulin than an intravenous glucose load that produces the same blood glucose concentration. Which hormone is mainly responsible?","choices":{"A":"GIP from K cells","B":"CCK from I cells","C":"Secretin from S cells","D":"Gastrin from G cells","E":"Ghrelin from the stomach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oral glucose stimulates GIP release from K cells, and GIP binds receptors on pancreatic beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion; GLP-1 from L cells contributes. Intravenous glucose bypasses the gut and relies on direct beta cell sensing. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are not incretins in the lecture. Ghrelin increases appetite and is suppressed by food. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and is stimulated by oral glucose (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: oral glucose produces a larger insulin response than intravenous glucose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"The greater insulin response to oral glucose than to an equivalent intravenous glucose load has which name?","choices":{"A":"Somogyi effect","B":"Incretin effect","C":"Dawn phenomenon","D":"Glucose toxicity","E":"Insulin resistance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The larger insulin release after oral glucose is the incretin effect, mediated by GIP and GLP-1. The Somogyi effect is rebound hyperglycemia after nocturnal hypoglycemia. The dawn phenomenon is early morning hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormones. Glucose toxicity is beta cell impairment from chronic hyperglycemia. Insulin resistance is a reduced tissue response to insulin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are incretins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides GIP, the incretin GLP-1 amplifies insulin release. Which cells secrete GLP-1?","choices":{"A":"K cells of duodenum and jejunum","B":"I cells of duodenum and jejunum","C":"S cells of the duodenum","D":"L cells of ileum and colon","E":"G cells of the gastric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GLP-1 is cleaved from proglucagon and secreted by L cells of the distal ileum and colon in response to carbohydrates and fats. K cells secrete GIP. I cells secrete CCK. S cells secrete secretin. Antral G cells secrete gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, decreases gastric emptying, and decreases appetite (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs promote weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin from antral D cells diffuses to neighboring G cells. What effect does it have on them?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastrin release","B":"Inhibits gastrin release","C":"Stimulates histamine release","D":"Blocks gastrin receptors","E":"Destroys G cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Somatostatin binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin synthesis and release. Stimulation of gastrin release is produced by peptides, distension, and vagal GRP. Somatostatin also inhibits, rather than stimulates, histamine release. It acts on G cells, not on gastrin receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits G cells without destroying them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin decreases gastric acid and pepsinogen secretion and many hormones (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide is a somatostatin analog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, D cells release somatostatin in response to which luminal condition?","choices":{"A":"High gastric pH","B":"Gastric distension","C":"Low gastric pH","D":"Luminal amino acids","E":"Vagal GRP release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"D cells release somatostatin in response to low luminal pH, providing negative feedback that suppresses gastrin and acid. A high gastric pH, distension, luminal amino acids, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release instead. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (low pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits parietal and G cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin is increased by acid and decreased by vagal stimulation (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: this feedback is lost in gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Histamine from ECL cells increases parietal cell acid secretion through which receptor and second messenger?","choices":{"A":"H2 receptor and cAMP","B":"H1 receptor and IP3","C":"CCK2 receptor and calcium","D":"M3 receptor and calcium","E":"SST receptor and Gi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and drive the H+/K+-ATPase. H1 receptors act through IP3 in other tissues. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors through calcium. ACh acts on M3 receptors through calcium. A somatostatin receptor coupled to Gi lowers cAMP, which is how somatostatin inhibits acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (ECL cell histamine and D cell somatostatin pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine potentiates the acid stimulating effects of ACh and gastrin (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce acid stimulated by all three secretagogues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide rises before meals and during fasting to increase food intake?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric oxyntic cells before meals and during fasting and is orexigenic. Leptin from adipocytes, PYY from L cells, insulin from beta cells, and serotonin are anorexigenic in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, hormones and peptides influencing appetite).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in the fasting state and in Prader-Willi syndrome and decreased after gastric bypass (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin also increases gastric motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Leptin is an anorexigenic signal secreted in proportion to body fat mass. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes","B":"Enterocytes","C":"Beta cells","D":"Myocytes","E":"Adipocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and activates POMC neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons. Hepatocytes and myocytes are not listed sources of appetite peptides. Enterocytes do not secrete leptin, although L and I cells of the intestine secrete PYY, GLP-1, and CCK. Beta cells secrete insulin, another anorexigenic signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin and insulin stimulate the anorexigenic pathway (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: leptin rises with fat mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"In the orexigenic pathway, AGRP released from arcuate neurons acts at melanocortin-4 receptors in the paraventricular nucleus. How does it act there?","choices":{"A":"Competitive antagonist","B":"Partial receptor agonist","C":"Full receptor agonist","D":"Positive allosteric enhancer","E":"Irreversible agonist"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AGRP is a competitive antagonist at MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus, blocking satiety signaling while NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus. Partial, full, and irreversible agonists would activate MC4R and reduce appetite, which is the role of alpha-MSH. A positive allosteric enhancer would increase MC4R signaling. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MC4R defects cause hyperphagia and early onset obesity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: AGRP and NPY increase appetite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with reflux takes a drug that blocks receptors for a substance released by ECL cells. Which mode of signaling does the drug interrupt?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine diffusion","B":"Endocrine circulation","C":"Neurocrine release","D":"Autocrine feedback","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine from ECL cells diffuses through interstitial fluid to adjacent parietal cells, which is paracrine signaling. Endocrine signaling uses the portal and systemic circulation. Neurocrine signaling is release by neurons into a synapse. Autocrine signaling acts on the releasing cell itself. Luminal secretion releases products into the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptor antagonists block histamine on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers are used for reflux and peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman eats a fatty meal. A peptide from duodenal I cells contracts her gallbladder. How is this peptide classified?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine","B":"Autocrine","C":"Neurocrine","D":"GI hormone","E":"Exocrine enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CCK is secreted by I cells into the portal circulation, passes through the liver into the systemic circulation, and acts on distant targets, so it is a GI hormone. Paracrines act locally by diffusion. Neurocrines are released by neurons at synapses. Autocrines act on the cell that secretes them. Exocrine enzymes are secreted into the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrointestinal hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the four official GI hormones are gastrin, CCK, secretin, and GIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: CCK release explains postprandial biliary colic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man in a physiology study has electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve, and his serum gastrin rises even after atropine. The mediator is a peptide released by an action potential. How is this mediator classified?","choices":{"A":"Circulating hormone","B":"Paracrine peptide","C":"Neurocrine peptide","D":"Autocrine signal","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastrin-releasing peptide is released by postganglionic vagal and enteric neurons onto G cells after an action potential, so it is a neurocrine; it is not cholinergic, which is why atropine does not block it. Circulating hormones travel in the blood. Paracrines are released by endocrine cells and diffuse locally. Autocrine signals act on the releasing cell. Luminal secretions enter the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation increases gastrin through GRP (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GRP is also called bombesin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old man has a study in which acidic chyme with a pH of 4 is infused into his duodenum. His pancreatic juice volume and bicarbonate concentration rise. Which hormone is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin","B":"Motilin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Secretin","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acid below pH 4.5 in the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which stimulates pancreatic ductal and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Gastrin release is inhibited by acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives migrating motor complexes. Ghrelin increases appetite. Somatostatin is also released by acid but inhibits pancreatic secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin, motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- and decreases gastric acid (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin is secreted by S cells in the duodenal mucosa (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — secretin and duodenal acid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has hepatobiliary scintigraphy and receives an analog of the duodenal hormone that contracts the gallbladder. Which additional effect does this hormone produce?","choices":{"A":"Faster gastric emptying","B":"Increased gastric acid","C":"Inhibition of enzyme release","D":"Contraction of Oddi sphincter","E":"Slower gastric emptying"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and delays gastric emptying so that fat enters the duodenum slowly. Faster emptying is the opposite effect. Increased gastric acid is an action of gastrin. CCK relaxes rather than contracts the sphincter of Oddi. CCK stimulates, rather than inhibits, enzyme release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: CCK analogs are used to measure gallbladder ejection fraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a steak dinner, and his serum gastrin rises within minutes. Which luminal component stimulates his antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"Intact dietary triglycerides","B":"Small peptides and amino acids","C":"Free fatty acids and monoglycerides","D":"Luminal acid below pH 3","E":"Glucose and galactose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small peptides and amino acids in the stomach, distension, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release. Intact triglycerides do not release gastrin. Fatty acids and monoglycerides in the duodenum release CCK. Acid below pH 3 inhibits gastrin release. Glucose stimulates GIP rather than gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is increased by distension, alkalinization, amino acids, peptides, and vagal GRP, and decreased by gastric acid; the lecture uses pH below 3, while First Aid gives pH below 1.5 (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: protein meals raise gastrin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — gastric phase of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has ulcers in the distal duodenum, chronic diarrhea, and weight loss. His fasting serum gastrin is markedly elevated while his gastric pH is low. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastrinoma","B":"Insulinoma","C":"VIPoma","D":"Somatostatinoma","E":"Glucagonoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal ulcers with high gastrin and low gastric pH indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, caused by a gastrinoma. An insulinoma causes hypoglycemia. A VIPoma causes watery diarrhea with achlorhydria, so gastric pH would be high. A somatostatinoma is a delta cell tumor that does not raise gastrin. A glucagonoma causes diabetes and necrolytic migratory erythema. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, responsible tumor); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gastrinoma is a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: gastrinomas often lie in the gastrinoma triangle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man has a gastrinoma with a gastric pH near 1. In a healthy stomach, this acidity would sharply reduce gastrin release. Why does his gastrin stay high?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin excess","B":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","C":"Loss of histamine receptors","D":"Tumor ignores pH feedback","E":"Vagal hyperactivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin from the tumor is not subject to negative feedback by low gastric pH, so acid secretion becomes massive and autonomous. Somatostatin excess would lower gastrin. Parietal cell autoimmunity raises gastrin by removing acid, which is the opposite pH. Loss of histamine receptors would reduce acid. Vagal hyperactivity increases gastrin through GRP but would still be subject to acid feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, H+ secretion effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is decreased by gastric acid in normal physiology (lecture pH below 3; First Aid pH below 1.5) and increased in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: treatment uses proton pump inhibitors and tumor resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — feedback control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers despite treatment. Endoscopy shows prominent thickened folds in the gastric body, and his serum gastrin is markedly elevated. Which mechanism explains the thickened folds?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin induced edema","B":"Trophic effect of gastrin","C":"Lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Foveolar mucous hyperplasia","E":"Submucosal fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronically elevated gastrin from a gastrinoma has a trophic effect that causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa, including parietal cell hyperplasia, which thickens the folds. Gastrin acts by growth of the mucosa, not by edema. Lymphocytic infiltration is a feature of gastritis or lymphoma. Foveolar mucous hyperplasia without high gastrin is Ménétrier disease. Submucosal fibrosis follows ulcer healing, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin stimulates trophic growth of the gastric oxyntic mucosa (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: refractory ulcers with thick folds warrant a fasting gastrin level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a fasting study has a normal plasma glucose. He receives an intravenous infusion of GIP with no glucose. How does his insulin secretion respond?","choices":{"A":"Large rise causing hypoglycemia","B":"Fall below the fasting level","C":"Rise matching an oral glucose load","D":"Little change from fasting level","E":"Delayed rise after several hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GIP stimulates insulin secretion from beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner: it amplifies glucose-induced insulin release, so with normal fasting glucose and no glucose load, insulin changes little. A large rise with hypoglycemia would require insulin release independent of glucose. GIP does not suppress insulin below fasting levels. A rise matching an oral glucose load needs both the glucose stimulus and the incretins. A delayed rise after several hours is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP stimulates insulin in a glucose-dependent manner, p29; section 4, incretins amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion, p29).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP is the only GI hormone released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 act through G-protein coupled receptors on beta cells (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GIP release and actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts a weekly injected analog of the incretin secreted by ileal L cells. Besides improving insulin secretion, which effect helps her lose weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased ghrelin","B":"Increased glucagon release","C":"Reduced appetite","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower leptin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GLP-1 analogs amplify glucose-dependent insulin release, slow gastric emptying, and are anorexigenic, reducing appetite. Increased ghrelin would promote weight gain. GLP-1 inhibits rather than increases glucagon release. GLP-1 slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. Lower leptin would increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4 and 6, GLP-1); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes and promote weight loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nausea from slowed gastric emptying is a common adverse effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormones that slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman eats a mixed meal, and her duodenal K cells release a hormone that amplifies her insulin secretion. Which other action does this hormone have?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastric acid","B":"Contracts the gallbladder","C":"Speeds gastric emptying","D":"Increases appetite","E":"Inhibits gastric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GIP from K cells stimulates insulin release and also inhibits gastric H+ secretion, inhibits gastrin, and slows gastric motility. Stimulation of acid is an action of gastrin. Gallbladder contraction is an action of CCK. GIP slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. GIP is listed as anorexigenic, so it does not increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP, and section 6, anorexigenic hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP decreases gastric H+ secretion and increases insulin release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP was first named gastric inhibitory peptide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — inhibitors of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with acromegaly starts octreotide, an analog of a D cell paracrine. Her serum gastrin falls. Which mechanism explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of G cells","B":"Inhibition of parietal cells","C":"Blockade of H2 receptors","D":"Rise in gastric pH","E":"Loss of vagal GRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Octreotide mimics somatostatin, which binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin release. Inhibition of parietal cells lowers acid but would raise, not lower, gastrin through loss of acid feedback. Blockade of H2 receptors is the action of H2 antagonists. A rise in gastric pH would increase gastrin. Octreotide does not remove vagal GRP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is a somatostatin analog used for acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, VIPoma, and variceal bleeding (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide can cause gallstones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — somatostatin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman takes a drug that blocks histamine receptors on parietal cells, and her acid secretion falls. Which second messenger falls in her parietal cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Cyclic AMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cyclic AMP and protein kinase A activity, which drives the H+/K+-ATPase; blocking H2 receptors lowers cyclic AMP. IP3 and calcium mediate ACh and gastrin. Cyclic GMP and nitric oxide mediate smooth muscle relaxation. Diacylglycerol accompanies IP3 in the calcium pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptors use a Gs cascade to increase H+/K+-ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce nocturnal acid secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — H2 receptor antagonists","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has antral H. pylori gastritis and a duodenal ulcer. His serum gastrin is elevated and his gastric acid output is high. Loss or suppression of which gastric cell best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"D cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Chief cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antral gastritis causes loss or suppression of D cell somatostatin (Bootcamp describes D cell destruction; Costanzo describes inhibited somatostatin secretion), which removes inhibition of G cells and parietal cells and raises gastrin and acid. Loss of G cells would lower gastrin. Loss of parietal cells would lower acid. Loss of ECL cells would lower histamine and acid. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and do not control gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (destruction of antral D cells lowers somatostatin and raises acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are associated with H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: eradicating H. pylori lowers ulcer recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has severe hyperphagia and obesity, and his serum leptin is undetectable because of a gene mutation. Which change in his arcuate nucleus explains his hunger?","choices":{"A":"Less POMC neuron activity","B":"Less AGRP neuron activity","C":"Less NPY neuron activity","D":"More MC4R activation","E":"Lower ghrelin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin activates POMC and CART neurons and inhibits NPY and AGRP neurons. Without leptin, POMC activity falls, alpha-MSH no longer activates MC4R, and NPY and AGRP activity rises, increasing appetite. Less AGRP activity, less NPY activity, and more MC4R activation would reduce appetite. Lower ghrelin would also reduce hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (leptin stimulates the anorexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin activates POMC and CART neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with Prader-Willi syndrome has constant hunger, and her plasma ghrelin is markedly elevated. Which arcuate neurons does ghrelin activate to increase her food intake?","choices":{"A":"POMC and CART neurons","B":"NPY and AGRP neurons","C":"Lateral hypothalamic neurons","D":"Paraventricular MC4R neurons","E":"Brainstem NTS neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ghrelin stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus; NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus and AGRP blocks MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus. POMC and CART neurons are anorexigenic and are activated by leptin and insulin. The lateral hypothalamus and paraventricular nucleus are downstream targets, not arcuate neurons. The NTS receives MC4R projections in the satiety pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin rises with fasting and sleep deprivation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman reports much less hunger after a gastric bypass operation. Which hormonal change most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Higher orexin release","B":"Lower PYY levels","C":"Higher NPY release","D":"Reduced leptin action","E":"Lower ghrelin levels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass, reducing stimulation of NPY and AGRP neurons and lowering hunger. Higher orexin release, higher NPY release, and reduced leptin action would increase hunger, because orexins and NPY are orexigenic. PYY is anorexigenic, so lower PYY would also increase hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, appetite hormones); First Aid 2025 GI (ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass surgery (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: PYY decreases appetite by inhibiting ghrelin secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has a tumor of the cells that secrete the GI paracrine somatostatin. Besides the GI mucosa and hypothalamus, where do these cells normally reside?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islets","B":"Adrenal medulla","C":"Anterior pituitary","D":"Thyroid follicles","E":"Renal cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin is secreted by D cells throughout the GI mucosa, by delta cells of the pancreatic islets, and by the hypothalamus. The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines. The anterior pituitary is a target of hypothalamic somatostatin, which inhibits growth hormone, rather than a source. Thyroid follicles make thyroid hormone. The renal cortex is not a somatostatin source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from pancreatic delta cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits growth hormone release from the anterior pituitary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman in a pancreatic function study receives secretin, and her bicarbonate output rises modestly. Adding CCK raises bicarbonate output much further. Which action of CCK explains this?","choices":{"A":"Stimulation of gastrin release","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Potentiation of secretin","D":"Inhibition of secretin release","E":"Stimulation of ghrelin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK potentiates secretin-induced pancreatic bicarbonate secretion, so the two together produce more bicarbonate than secretin by itself. CCK does not stimulate gastrin release or inhibit secretin release. CCK is anorexigenic, so it does not stimulate ghrelin. Gallbladder contraction is a real CCK action, but it does not raise pancreatic bicarbonate output. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin mainly raises pancreatic bicarbonate while CCK mainly raises enzyme output (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and secretin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypercalcemia from parathyroid hyperplasia and peptic ulcers that do not heal with treatment. His fasting gastrin is high and his gastric pH is low. Which test best confirms the suspected tumor?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucose tolerance test","B":"Urea breath test","C":"Serum glucagon level","D":"Secretin stimulation test","E":"Dexamethasone suppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refractory ulcers with high gastrin, low gastric pH, and MEN1 features suggest a gastrinoma, and the secretin stimulation test follows fasting gastrin and gastric pH in the workup. An oral glucose tolerance test evaluates diabetes. A urea breath test detects H. pylori. A serum glucagon level is used for glucagonoma. Dexamethasone suppression evaluates Cushing syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma diagnostics: fasting serum gastrin and gastric pH, then secretin stimulation test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor in the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: screen for MEN1 in a patient with gastrinoma and hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":3,"stem":"In a 74-year-old man, oral glucose releases gut incretins that amplify insulin secretion. Through which receptor type do these incretins act on his pancreatic beta cells?","choices":{"A":"G protein coupled receptors","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinases","C":"Ligand gated ion channels","D":"Nuclear hormone receptors","E":"JAK STAT cytokine receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GIP and GLP-1 bind G protein coupled receptors on beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion. Receptor tyrosine kinases mediate insulin action on target tissues. Ligand gated ion channels mediate fast synaptic transmission. Nuclear hormone receptors bind steroid and thyroid hormones. JAK STAT receptors bind cytokines and growth hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral glucose releases GIP and GLP 1 from intestinal K and L cells (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: incretins amplify insulin release in a glucose-dependent manner.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman takes famotidine, and her acid response to a protein meal falls. Which signals normally stimulate release of the paracrine whose action this drug opposes?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Gastrin and ACh","C":"Somatostatin and PGE2","D":"Leptin and insulin","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ECL cells release histamine in response to gastrin and ACh, and histamine then binds H2 receptors on parietal cells and potentiates the direct effects of ACh and gastrin. Secretin and GIP inhibit acid secretion. Somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit acid secretion. Leptin and insulin are anorexigenic signals. VIP and nitric oxide relax GI smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acid secretion is stimulated by ACh, gastrin, and histamine and inhibited by somatostatin, secretin, prostaglandins, and GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cells). COMLEX: H2 blockers blunt acid responses to meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has early onset obesity and hyperphagia from a loss of function mutation in melanocortin-4 receptors. Besides increased appetite, which change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sympathetic tone","B":"Lower ghrelin secretion","C":"Higher leptin sensitivity","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower energy expenditure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MC4R activation in the paraventricular nucleus decreases food intake and increases energy expenditure through projections to the NTS and increased sympathetic activity. Loss of MC4R function therefore lowers energy expenditure and sympathetic tone. Lower ghrelin would reduce appetite. MC4R loss does not increase leptin sensitivity, since leptin acts upstream of MC4R. Gastric emptying is not an MC4R effect in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: alpha-MSH acts at MC4R to promote satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl05_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has new diabetes and gallstones and no prior abdominal surgery. CT shows a mass in the pancreas, and she has lost weight. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Fecal fat"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↓","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↔"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"A somatostatinoma releases excess somatostatin from tumor delta cells, so the pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is correct. Somatostatin suppresses gastrin release from G cells and inhibits parietal cell acid secretion, causing hypochlorhydria, and it inhibits pancreatic secretion, causing steatorrhea; the diabetes and gallstones reflect inhibition of insulin release and gallbladder contraction. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is a gastrinoma, which raises gastrin and acid, and the acid entering the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase and causes steatorrhea. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) follows resection of the gastric antrum, which removes the G cells so gastrin and acid fall. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is a glucagonoma, which causes diabetes, weight loss, and necrolytic migratory erythema without changing gastric secretion. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is autoimmune atrophic gastritis, in which loss of parietal cells lowers acid and removes feedback on G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion; section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; acid inactivates pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Robbins (SDL 19 pathology text: somatostatinomas cause diabetes, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors: gastrinoma steatorrhea, glucagonoma, and somatostatinoma); First Aid 2025 GI (somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin release). Rows C, D, and E list ↔ for analytes their sources do not describe as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin and glucagon release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide, a somatostatin analog, can cause gallstones and steatorrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}],"6":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which sensory receptors detect the bolus and initiate the involuntary swallowing reflex?","choices":{"A":"Gastric stretch receptors","B":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors","C":"Laryngeal chemoreceptors","D":"Esophageal pain receptors","E":"Tonsillar pillar receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tactile receptors around the pharyngeal opening, especially on the tonsillar pillars, detect the bolus pushed back by the tongue and start the automatic swallowing reflex. Gastric stretch receptors drive gastric reflexes. Carotid sinus baroreceptors sense blood pressure. Laryngeal chemoreceptors trigger cough, not swallowing. Esophageal pain receptors do not start swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (oral phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to neural or striated muscle disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves carry the afferent limb of the swallowing reflex from the pharynx to the medulla?","choices":{"A":"CN VII and CN XII","B":"CN X and CN XI","C":"CN III and CN IV","D":"CN V and CN IX","E":"CN VI and CN XII"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory impulses from pharyngeal tactile receptors travel in the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves to the tractus solitarius in the medullary swallowing center. The other nerve pairs are not the afferent limb in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, involuntary pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center lies in the medulla (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: brainstem strokes impair swallowing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"During the pharyngeal stage of swallowing, which event prevents food from refluxing into the nasal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Epiglottis tilting back","B":"Soft palate elevation","C":"Vocal cord closure","D":"Upper sphincter relaxation","E":"Tongue base retraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The soft palate elevates to close the posterior nares and prevent reflux into the nasal cavity. The epiglottis swings back and the vocal cords approximate to protect the airway, not the nose. Relaxation of the upper esophageal sphincter opens the esophagus. Tongue movement pushes the bolus back in the voluntary stage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary esophageal peristalsis is initiated by which event?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal distension","B":"Refluxed gastric acid","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Duodenal fat delivery","E":"A voluntary swallow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary peristalsis begins with the voluntary act of swallowing as a continuation of the pharyngeal peristaltic wave, coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Esophageal distension and refluxed gastric contents initiate secondary peristalsis. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response to the bolus. Duodenal fat slows gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES opens and the orad stomach relaxes as the primary wave travels down (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: primary waves propel the swallowed bolus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does primary peristalsis take to carry a bolus through the esophagus in an upright person?","choices":{"A":"5 to 8 seconds","B":"8 to 10 seconds","C":"1 to 2 seconds","D":"20 to 30 seconds","E":"60 to 90 seconds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Transit takes about 5 to 8 seconds upright because gravity assists the wave. Lying down or stationary, it takes about 8 to 10 seconds. The pharyngeal stage, not esophageal transit, lasts under 2 seconds. Twenty seconds or more is far longer than normal transit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gravity accelerates esophageal transit when sitting or standing (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: delayed transit suggests a motility disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, secondary esophageal peristalsis can still occur after the vagal supply is interrupted. Which mechanism allows this?","choices":{"A":"Medullary swallowing center","B":"Intrinsic myenteric circuits","C":"Somatic motor reflex arcs","D":"Sympathetic chain ganglia input","E":"Voluntary cortical input"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intrinsic myenteric networks can drive secondary peristaltic waves on their own after a brief period of adaptation, even when vagal supply is interrupted. The medullary swallowing center acts through the vagus and is cut off. Somatic motor reflexes do not drive smooth muscle peristalsis. Sympathetic input inhibits motility. Voluntary cortical input starts primary peristalsis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary wave is mediated by the enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis clears residual food and refluxed contents (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired clearance prolongs acid exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, interstitial cells of Cajal in the caudad stomach generate slow waves at about what rate?","choices":{"A":"12 per minute","B":"8 to 10 per minute","C":"1 per minute","D":"3 to 5 per minute","E":"20 to 30 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves of about 3 to 5 per minute in the caudad stomach. About 12 per minute is the duodenal rate in Costanzo. Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 per minute match no gastric source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm); Costanzo Ch. 8 (slow wave frequency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials; action potentials on top of them cause phasic contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastric pacing disorders slow emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term retropulsion describe in gastric motility?","choices":{"A":"Antral contents jetting backward","B":"Chyme entering the duodenum","C":"Bile refluxing into the stomach","D":"Fundic receptive relaxation","E":"Duodenal acid inhibiting emptying"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As a peristaltic ring nears the closed pylorus, most antral contents are squirted backward through the ring toward the body, mixing them with acid. Chyme entering the duodenum is emptying. Bile reflux is retrograde duodenal flow. Receptive relaxation is fundic accommodation. Duodenal acid acts through enterogastric feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach mixes and grinds, and the orad stomach receives the bolus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: solids leave the stomach more slowly than liquids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does the circular muscle wall of the pylorus compare with the antral wall before it?","choices":{"A":"About the same thickness","B":"10 to 20 percent thicker","C":"50 to 100 percent thicker","D":"Two to three times thinner","E":"Five times thicker"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pyloric sphincter is 50% to 100% thicker than the preceding antral wall. Equal thickness, 10 to 20 percent, thinner walls, and five times thicker do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is hypertrophy of this circular muscle (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis presents with nonbilious projectile vomiting at about 2 to 6 weeks of age (First Aid 2025 GI; Robbins and the lecture give the third to sixth weeks).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Besides slowing antral propulsion, which effect does it produce?","choices":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion","B":"Faster fundic contraction","C":"Relaxed pyloric sphincter","D":"Increased gastric acid","E":"Increased pyloric tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK slows antral propulsion and increases pyloric sphincter tone, giving time for bile emulsification and pancreatic digestion of fat. Increased antral propulsion and faster fundic contraction would speed emptying. A relaxed pyloric sphincter would let chyme pass faster. CCK does not act by raising gastric acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (CCK inhibits gastric emptying).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals stay in the stomach longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how quickly do enterogastric reflexes act after duodenal stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Within 5 minutes","B":"Within 2 hours","C":"Within 30 seconds","D":"Within 30 minutes","E":"Within 24 hours"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Neural enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall act within 30 seconds through myenteric, sympathetic, and vagal routes. Five minutes, 30 minutes, and longer intervals are slower than these neural reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal feedback keeps chyme delivery within intestinal digestive and buffering capacity (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: rapid emptying overwhelms the duodenum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"Acid that lowers duodenal pH below 4.5 releases a hormone that inhibits gastric motility and acid secretion. Which hormone and source is this?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin from EC cells","B":"GIP from K cells","C":"Gastrin from G cells","D":"Secretin from S cells","E":"Motilin from M cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Duodenal S cells release secretin in response to acid below pH 4.5; secretin inhibits gastrin release, gastric acid secretion, and gastric motility until bicarbonate neutralizes the chyme. Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates enteric sensory neurons rather than acting as this duodenal hormone. GIP is released by fat, amino acids, and carbohydrate. Gastrin stimulates acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives interdigestive motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP exerts a mild inhibitory effect on gastric motility and emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal acid slows gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what is the resting pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg","B":"100 mm Hg","C":"60 mm Hg","D":"Minus 5 mm Hg","E":"30 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The resting LES is tonically contracted at about 30 mm Hg, which overcomes the negative intrathoracic pressure of the middle esophagus. Five mm Hg would be too low to prevent reflux. A negative value describes intrathoracic esophageal pressure. Values of 100 and 60 mm Hg are higher than normal resting tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: smoking, alcohol, and caffeine are GERD risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides extrinsic pinch valve reinforcement of the lower esophageal sphincter during coughing or straining?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal rectus muscles","B":"Diaphragmatic crura","C":"Upper esophageal sphincter","D":"Intercostal muscles","E":"Pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Skeletal muscle of the diaphragmatic crura surrounds the LES at the esophageal hiatus and reinforces it during inspiration, coughing, and straining. Rectus muscles raise abdominal pressure but do not encircle the LES. The upper esophageal sphincter keeps air out of the upper esophagus. Intercostal muscles move the chest wall. The pyloric sphincter controls gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction into the mediastinum and raises GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, increased intra-abdominal pressure helps prevent reflux by compressing which structure?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophageal segment","B":"Thoracic esophageal body","C":"Wall of the gastric fundus","D":"Upper esophageal sphincter","E":"Pyloric sphincter muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 1 to 2 cm segment of distal esophagus lies in the abdomen, so rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses it inward, creating a flap valve closure. The thoracic esophagus is exposed to intrathoracic pressure. Compression of the fundus would push contents upward. The upper esophageal sphincter and pylorus are not compressed by intra-abdominal pressure in this mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal esophageal segment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1 to 2 cm abdominal esophagus is compressed by intra-abdominal pressure (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity or pregnancy and hiatal hernia still promote GERD (lecture SDL 8; Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has a stroke in the medulla, and liquids enter his trachea during swallowing. Failure of which pharyngeal stage event best explains the aspiration?","choices":{"A":"Soft palate elevation","B":"Lower sphincter relaxation","C":"Secondary esophageal waves","D":"Gastric receptive relaxation","E":"Airway closure by larynx"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the pharyngeal stage the vocal cords approximate, the larynx moves up and forward, and the epiglottis covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration; this sequence is coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Soft palate elevation protects the nasal cavity. LES relaxation, secondary peristalsis, and receptive relaxation occur after the bolus leaves the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center coordinates CN V, IX, X, and XII (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: aspiration risk is high after brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — brainstem nuclei of vagal reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has anterior neck surgery that tethers the hyoid, and food sticks at the top of his esophagus. Which normal event opens the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"VIP release at the LES","B":"Laryngeal stretch","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Secondary peristalsis","E":"Epiglottic closure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upward laryngeal movement stretches and relaxes the upper esophageal sphincter, opening the entrance to the esophagus. VIP release relaxes the lower, not upper, sphincter. Secondary peristalsis begins in the esophagus after the bolus enters. Receptive relaxation occurs in the orad stomach. Epiglottic closure protects the airway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (upper esophageal sphincter relaxes in the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired laryngeal elevation causes oropharyngeal dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — striated muscle of the upper esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has a videofluoroscopic swallow study, and his breathing pauses briefly with each swallow. Which mechanism causes the pause?","choices":{"A":"Voluntary cortical breath holding","B":"Reflex vocal cord paralysis","C":"Medullary respiratory inhibition","D":"Fatigue of the phrenic nerve","E":"Nasal block by the epiglottis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medullary signals temporarily inhibit respiration throughout the pharyngeal stage, which lasts under 2 seconds. The pause is reflex, not voluntary. The vocal cords approximate actively rather than becoming paralyzed. Phrenic nerve fatigue would not cycle with each swallow. The soft palate, not the epiglottis, closes the nasal passage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pharyngeal phase is a reflex controlled in the medulla (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired coordination of breathing and swallowing raises aspiration risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old woman has nighttime reflux, and esophageal manometry records a peristaltic wave that begins in the midesophagus without a swallow. What started this wave?","choices":{"A":"A voluntary pharyngeal swallow","B":"Distension of the wall","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Cortical motor command","E":"Duodenal acid reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secondary peristalsis is started locally by distension of the esophageal wall from residual food or refluxed gastric contents and does not require a swallow. A voluntary swallow starts primary peristalsis in the pharynx. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response. A cortical command is part of the voluntary stage. Duodenal acid acts on gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6; Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nocturnal reflux is cleared by secondary waves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — intrinsic enteric circuits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man swallows a large bolus that the primary wave does not fully clear. A second wave clears it without another swallow. Where does this second wave begin?","choices":{"A":"In the upper pharyngeal wall","B":"At the lower sphincter","C":"In the gastric fundus","D":"At the site of distension","E":"At the cricopharyngeus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distension and travels downward until the lumen is cleared. Waves starting in the pharynx are primary waves. The lower sphincter relaxes rather than starting the wave. The gastric fundus is beyond the esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is part of the upper esophageal sphincter. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distention); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: impaired secondary peristalsis worsens reflux esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — the peristaltic reflex","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a 24-hour pH and manometry study. During one reflux episode, refluxed acid distends his midesophagus while he is not swallowing. Which pattern of esophageal motor activity follows?","choices":{"A":"A single wave followed by quiescence","B":"Simultaneous contraction of the whole body","C":"Retrograde waves toward the pharynx","D":"Tonic contraction of the esophageal body","E":"Repeated waves until the lumen clears"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distension from refluxed contents starts secondary peristalsis without a swallow, and secondary waves repeat until the esophageal lumen is fully cleared. A single wave followed by quiescence would leave residual refluxate when one wave fails to clear it. Simultaneous contraction of the whole body is not a clearing pattern. Peristaltic waves travel caudally toward the stomach, not toward the pharynx. A tonic contraction of the esophageal body would not propel the refluxate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared, p33; section 5, secondary clearance of reflux, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated partly by myenteric circuits and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: local distension during reflux triggers secondary waves that return fluid to the stomach (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a gastric emptying study. Solid food leaves his stomach only after it has been broken into particles smaller than 1 mm. Which mechanism grinds the solids?","choices":{"A":"Fundic relaxation","B":"Duodenal segmentation","C":"Antral retropulsion","D":"Migrating complexes","E":"Pyloric relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strong antral rings push contents against a nearly closed pylorus, so most chyme is squirted back toward the body, shearing solids into particles under 1 mm. Fundic relaxation stores the meal. Duodenal segmentation mixes chyme after it leaves the stomach. Migrating motor complexes clear the fasting stomach. Pyloric relaxation would let solids leave without grinding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach has the thickest muscle and produces the strongest contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired antral grinding delays solid emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — circular and longitudinal muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has a gastric emptying study. Liquid leaves her stomach quickly, while solid food stays until it is mixed into chyme. Which feature explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Tonic pyloric contraction","B":"Weak tone in the fundus","C":"Faster gastric slow waves","D":"Acidic duodenal contents","E":"Absent antral peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter lets fluids pass easily but restricts solids until they are mixed into fluid chyme. Weak fundic tone would affect storage, not particle size. Faster slow waves would increase contraction rate for both phases. Duodenal acid slows emptying of all contents. Absent antral peristalsis would slow both liquids and solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric sphincter is circular smooth muscle (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: gastric emptying studies use radiolabeled solid meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — the pyloric circular muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has antral manometry after a meal. For about one fifth of the recording, powerful antral contractions generate 50 to 70 cm H2O of pressure. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"Mixing waves","B":"Pyloric pump","C":"Mass movement","D":"Segmentation","E":"Receptive relaxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For about 20% of the time after a meal, gastric peristalsis turns into powerful contractions called the pyloric pump, generating 50 to 70 cm H2O and driving emptying. Mixing waves are weak constrictor waves that do not empty the stomach. Mass movements occur in the colon. Segmentation mixes small intestinal contents. Receptive relaxation lowers orad gastric pressure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric pump generates 50 to 70 cm H2O and drives emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes inhibit the pyloric pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman feels full for hours after fatty meals but not after meals of fruit and bread. Which response in her duodenum most slows emptying after the fatty meal?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release by peptides","B":"GIP release by glucose","C":"CCK release by lipids","D":"Motilin release by fasting","E":"Ghrelin release by fasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fatty acids and monoglycerides release CCK from I cells, and CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Gastrin is released in the stomach and stimulates acid. GIP has only a mild inhibitory effect on emptying. Motilin and ghrelin rise during fasting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: low fat meals help patients with delayed emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — CCK","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man drinks a hyperosmolar sports drink, and it empties from his stomach more slowly than the same volume of water. Which mechanism slows emptying of the drink?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal osmoreceptor reflex","B":"Gastric acid hypersecretion","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Faster antral slow waves","E":"Vagal receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor osmolality, and hypertonic chyme triggers enterogastric reflexes that inhibit the pyloric pump and increase pyloric tone. Gastric acid secretion does not depend on drink osmolality. Pyloric relaxation and faster antral waves would speed emptying. Receptive relaxation accommodates both drinks equally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli and enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal distension, acid, osmolality, and fat and protein products slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: isotonic fluids empty faster than hypertonic ones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — reflex control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has a vagotomy, and duodenal distension still inhibits his antral contractions within seconds. Which routes of the enterogastric reflex remain?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent pathways","B":"Circulating gastrin release","C":"Pelvic splanchnic reflexes","D":"Somatic spinal reflexes","E":"Myenteric and sympathetic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterogastric reflexes act through intrinsic myenteric circuits, sympathetic nerves via prevertebral ganglia, and the vagus; after vagotomy the myenteric and sympathetic routes remain. Vagal efferents are cut. Gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric activity. Pelvic splanchnic fibers supply the distal colon. Somatic reflexes do not control gastric smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity decreases peristalsis and increases sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, general principles). COMLEX: vagotomy also removes receptive relaxation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic effects on motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a sliding hiatal hernia has daily heartburn. Her gastroesophageal junction lies above the diaphragm. Which antireflux mechanism has she lost?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment compression","B":"Esophageal cardiac gland mucus","C":"Secondary peristaltic clearance","D":"Upper sphincter tone","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the gastroesophageal junction moves into the mediastinum, the distal esophagus no longer lies in the abdomen, so intra-abdominal pressure no longer compresses it into a flap valve, and crural reinforcement is lost. Cardiac gland mucus and secondary peristalsis are separate clearance and buffering defenses. Upper sphincter tone keeps air out of the esophagus. Receptive relaxation is gastric accommodation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal segment and crural reinforcement); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (sliding hiatal hernia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sliding hiatal hernia is the most common type and increases GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity and pregnancy are risk factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — sliding hiatal hernia in GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has heartburn and regurgitation. Manometry shows normal resting sphincter pressure, and pH monitoring shows acid exposure episodes that coincide with periods of complete sphincter relaxation without a preceding swallow. Which mechanism explains his reflux?","choices":{"A":"Excess LES resting tone","B":"Absent gastric acid","C":"Strong crural contraction","D":"Transient LES relaxations","E":"Rapid gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Abnormal transient relaxations of the LES permit gastric reflux even when resting tone is normal. Excess resting tone would oppose reflux and suggests achalasia. Absent acid would reduce injury. Strong crural contraction reinforces the barrier. Rapid gastric emptying would lower the volume available to reflux. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD presents with burning retrosternal pain, regurgitation, and cough (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are first line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old woman in a manometry study has a zone at the gastroesophageal junction with pressure higher than in both the esophagus and the stomach. Which property produces this zone?","choices":{"A":"Cholinergic mucosal secretion","B":"Tonic circular contraction","C":"Longitudinal muscle shortening","D":"Submucosal venous engorgement","E":"Constant VIP release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The LES is circular smooth muscle at the distal esophagus that stays tonically contracted at rest, creating a pressure barrier above both intrathoracic esophageal and gastric pressure. Mucosal secretion does not generate sphincter pressure. Longitudinal shortening does not create a sphincter zone. Venous engorgement is not the mechanism. VIP relaxes the LES. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Costanzo Ch. 8 (LES resting pressure exceeds esophageal and gastric pressure).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES resting pressure exceeds intrathoracic and gastric pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: low LES pressure predisposes to GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has a small stroke involving the medullary nucleus that moves his tongue, and he cannot push a chewed bolus back toward his pharynx. Which cranial nerve carries this motor output?","choices":{"A":"Facial motor nerve","B":"Hypoglossal nerve","C":"Spinal accessory nerve","D":"Phrenic nerve","E":"Abducens nerve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The swallowing center coordinates motor output through cranial nerves V, IX, X, and XII and upper cervical nerves; the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) moves the tongue, which rolls the bolus back against the hard palate. The facial nerve is not in the lecture list of swallowing motor nerves. The spinal accessory nerve supplies neck and shoulder muscles. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The abducens nerve moves the eye. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary and pharyngeal stages).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tongue weakness causes oral phase dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-year-old woman has esophageal manometry during a swallow. At what point does her lower esophageal sphincter open?","choices":{"A":"Just after the UES closes","B":"After the bolus enters the stomach","C":"Before the pharyngeal stage","D":"After gastric contraction","E":"During exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The LES opens just after the upper esophageal sphincter returns to resting tone, so it is open for the whole time the bolus moves down the esophagus. Opening only after the bolus enters the stomach would block transit. Opening before the pharyngeal stage would precede the swallow. Gastric contraction is not the trigger. Respiratory phase does not time LES opening. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (esophageal motility, step 3: the LES opens just after the UES returns to resting tone); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary peristalsis: relaxation ahead of the advancing bolus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES stays open for the whole esophageal transit (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: failure of LES relaxation is achalasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — vagal transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman has gastric manometry after a meal. Where do the weak constrictor mixing waves of her stomach begin?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Midstomach","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weak peristaltic mixing waves originate in the midstomach and travel toward the antrum every 15 to 20 seconds, intensifying as they reach the thick caudad wall. The fundus is part of the thin walled orad stomach that stores food. The pyloric sphincter resists outflow. The duodenal bulb lies beyond the stomach. The cardia surrounds the gastroesophageal junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the orad stomach receives the meal and the caudad stomach mixes and grinds it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the caudad stomach wall is thickest.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — regions of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man has a gastric emptying study after a meal. Besides acid and fat, duodenal chyme with which property slows emptying?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic osmolality","B":"Neutral pH near 7","C":"Low fat and protein","D":"Small duodenal volume","E":"Hypotonic osmolality"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor distension, acidity, hypertonic or hypotonic osmolality, fat and protein products, and irritation, and each can slow emptying. Isotonic chyme, a neutral pH, low fat and protein content, and a small duodenal volume do not trigger this feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK, secretin, and GIP slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal irritation also slows gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — duodenal hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has esophageal manometry that maps his lower esophageal sphincter. About how far above the gastric junction does this sphincter zone extend?","choices":{"A":"About 10 cm","B":"About 25 cm","C":"About 0.5 cm","D":"About 3 cm","E":"About 15 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The LES is the circular smooth muscle at the distal end of the esophagus, extending about 3 cm above the junction with the stomach. Ten, 15, and 25 cm would include much of the thoracic esophagus. Half a centimeter is shorter than the sphincter zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES pressure exceeds gastric pressure at rest (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GERD presents with heartburn and regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman has antral recordings that show contractions occurring at a fixed maximum rate. What sets this maximum rate?","choices":{"A":"Slow wave frequency","B":"Vagal firing rate","C":"Serum gastrin level","D":"Pyloric resistance","E":"Fundic muscle tone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The frequency of slow waves generated by interstitial cells of Cajal sets the frequency of action potentials and therefore the maximum frequency of contractions. Vagal firing and gastrin change the number of action potentials and the force of each contraction, not the maximum rate. Pyloric resistance controls outflow. Fundic tone affects storage. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural and hormonal input changes the number of action potentials on each slow wave (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: stronger antral contractions speed gastric mixing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric pacemaking","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl06_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":4,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, tight thickened skin over her fingers, heartburn, and dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Esophageal manometry is performed. Which of the following sets of manometry findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["LES resting pressure","LES relaxation","Distal contractions"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↓"],"B":["↔","↔","↑"],"C":["↓","↔","↓"],"D":["↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis causes smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal esophagus, so the pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is correct. The weak sphincter loses the positive pressure barrier that normally prevents reflux, and the weak distal smooth muscle clears refluxed acid poorly, producing heartburn and dysphagia. The pattern (↑ LES resting pressure, ↓ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is achalasia, from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↑ distal contractions) is distal esophageal spasm, with uncoordinated high amplitude contractions. The pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is reflux from LES incompetence with intact peristalsis. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is cardiovascular dysphagia from external compression by an enlarged left atrium, which has normal manometry. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone and LES incompetence); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal disorders of motility: achalasia, distal esophageal spasm, cardiovascular dysphagia, scleroderma esophageal dysmotility; GERD); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia). Cells shown as ↔ are values the sources do not describe as changed; LES resting pressure in row D is inferred from the lecture term incompetence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma esophageal dysmotility shows ↓ LES resting pressure and ↓ esophageal contractions, and achalasia shows ↑ LES resting pressure with ↓ contractions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal disorders of motility). COMLEX: scleroderma is a risk factor for GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true}]};
